[2026-08-12 13:41:32] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN2 -> N3, N5\nN5 -> N6, N4\nN0 -> N8\nN9 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N5 leads to N6 (terminal) or N4 (terminal), both ending the game immediately. So from N5, the next player has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately.  \n- N9 leads to N2 or N7. From N2: to N3 (terminal) or N5 (which then ends in terminal). From N7: to N0 or N3; N0 leads to N8 (terminal), N3 to terminal. All paths from N9 eventually end. But the key is: if we go to N9, the opponent is forced into a path that may have a winning strategy. However, from N5, the next player has no legal move — they lose immediately. So moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.61553480475382, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N9. We analyze each:  \n- N5 leads to N6 (terminal) or N4 (terminal), both ending the game immediately. So from N5, the next player has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 wins immediately.  \n- N9 leads to N2 or N7. From N2: to N3 (terminal) or N5 (which then ends in terminal). From N7: to N0 or N3; N0 leads to N8 (terminal), N3 to terminal. All paths from N9 eventually end. But the key is: if we go to N9, the opponent is forced into a path that may have a winning strategy. However, from N5, the next player has no legal move — they lose immediately. So moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N5 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2, N8, N3\nN2 -> N4, N5, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6\nN3 -> N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N9\n\nLegal moves: N2, N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N2, N8, or N3. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 leads to N4, N5, or N7. N4 and N7 are terminal (opponent loses immediately), N5 leads to N6 or N9. N6 is terminal, N9 leads to N6 (terminal). So from N5, opponent eventually loses. So N2 leads to a winning position.  \n- N8 leads to N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.  \n- N3 leads to N2, which as above, leads to a winning position for the opponent. So N3 is bad.  \nThus, N8 is best: it directly leads to terminal (opponent loses immediately). This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.27625919637804197, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N2, N8, or N3. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N2 leads to N4, N5, or N7. N4 and N7 are terminal (opponent loses immediately), N5 leads to N6 or N9. N6 is terminal, N9 leads to N6 (terminal). So from N5, opponent eventually loses. So N2 leads to a winning position.  \n- N8 leads to N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.  \n- N3 leads to N2, which as above, leads to a winning position for the opponent. So N3 is bad.  \nThus, N8 is best: it directly leads to terminal (opponent loses immediately). This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2, N8, N3\nN2 -> N4, N5, N7\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6\nN3 -> N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N9\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N0 is the only option and results in an immediate win for the current player. Therefore, N0 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4931239388794567, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the only legal move is to N0. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N0 is the only option and results in an immediate win for the current player. Therefore, N0 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N3\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N9\nN2 -> N3, N5\nN5 -> N6, N4\nN0 -> N8\nN9 -> N2, N7\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6 and N4. Both N6 and N4 lead directly to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, either move ends the game with a win. Since both options lead to immediate victory, either is optimal. Choosing either is equivalent in outcome. However, to maximize outcome, we select one. N4 is a direct terminal move, as is N6. Both are valid and winning. Selecting N4 is sufficient.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4557724957555178, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6 and N4. Both N6 and N4 lead directly to terminal nodes, meaning the player who moves to them wins immediately because the opponent has no legal move. Thus, either move ends the game with a win. Since both options lead to immediate victory, either is optimal. Choosing either is equivalent in outcome. However, to maximize outcome, we select one. N4 is a direct terminal move, as is N6. Both are valid and winning. Selecting N4 is sufficient.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1, N6\nN9 -> N6, N2\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0\nN2 -> N5, N8\nN3 -> N9, N5, N7\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N5, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N9, N5, or N7. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N9 → N6 or N2:  \n  - N6 → terminal (win for the player who moves to N6).  \n  - N2 → N5 or N8: N5 leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal), N8 → terminal. So N2 leads to terminal.  \n  So from N9, the opponent eventually loses.  \n- N5 → N1 or N6: both terminal. So the opponent loses immediately.  \n- N7 → N0 → N4 → terminal. So opponent eventually loses.  \n\nAll moves from N3 lead to a terminal win for the current player. However, the goal is to maximize the outcome, meaning we must choose a move that ensures a win. Since all options lead to a win, any choice is valid. But choosing N5 gives immediate terminal options (N1 or N6), which are terminal, so the opponent has no move. This is a clean win. Thus, N5 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.32973967176004537, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N9, N5, or N7. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a losing position).  \n- N9 → N6 or N2:  \n  - N6 → terminal (win for the player who moves to N6).  \n  - N2 → N5 or N8: N5 leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal), N8 → terminal. So N2 leads to terminal.  \n  So from N9, the opponent eventually loses.  \n- N5 → N1 or N6: both terminal. So the opponent loses immediately.  \n- N7 → N0 → N4 → terminal. So opponent eventually loses.  \n\nAll moves from N3 lead to a terminal win for the current player. However, the goal is to maximize the outcome, meaning we must choose a move t