0 -> N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N12\nN12 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> N13, N5, N12\nN2 -> N9\nN5 -> N11, N8, N10\nN13 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0, N2, N1\nN4 -> N10\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N0, N2, or N1. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N0 → N6 → terminal: immediate win for the player who moves to N6; opponent loses.  \n- N2 → N9 → from N9, options are N13 (terminal), N5, N12. N5 leads to N11 or N8 or N10 (all terminal), so N5 ends quickly. N9 → N12 ends immediately. So, N2 leads to a branching point, but eventually all paths end.  \n- N1 → N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal): both end immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 leads directly to terminal on next move (N6), forcing opponent to lose. This is a winning move. N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.275410299943407, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N0, N2, or N1. We analyze each destination to determine which leads to a winning position.  \n- N0 → N6 → terminal: immediate win for the player who moves to N6; opponent loses.  \n- N2 → N9 → from N9, options are N13 (terminal), N5, N12. N5 leads to N11 or N8 or N10 (all terminal), so N5 ends quickly. N9 → N12 ends immediately. So, N2 leads to a branching point, but eventually all paths end.  \n- N1 → N7 (terminal) or N6 (terminal): both end immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N0 leads directly to terminal on next move (N6), forcing opponent to lose. This is a winning move. N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. 