N0 leads to N9 → N4 (terminal), so the opponent has no move after us — we win immediately. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.7534521788341821, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N0, N7, or N1. N0 leads to N9, then to N4 (terminal), so the opponent faces a terminal node after our move — we win. N7 leads to N0, N6, or N1; N6 and N1 are losing if the opponent plays optimally, but the game continues. N1 leads to N8 (terminal) or N2; N2 leads to N3 (terminal). We need to find a move that forces a win. N0 leads to N9 → N4 (terminal), so the opponent has no move after us — we win immediately. This is a winning move. Thus, choosing N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N3\nN7 -> N0, N6, N1\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N7, N1\nN0 -> N9\nN1 -> N8, N2\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the only legal move is to N9. From N9, the next move is to N4 (terminal), so the player who moves to N4 wins because the opponent has no move. However, N4 is terminal, meaning the player who moves to it has just completed a move and the next player cannot move. So, if the current player moves from N0 to N9, then from N9 to N4, the next player has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is to go to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.44473684210526315,