ot follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N6, N0\nN9 -> N2\nN7 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N3\nN4 -> N9, N5\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N6 and N8. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4999151103565365, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N6 and N8. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N9\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN4 -> N5, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N8\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N4.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N9, then to N2 or N8.  \n  - N2 → N1 (terminal) or N0 → N7 or N8.  \n  - N0 → N7 (terminal) or N8 (terminal).  \n  - Thus, from N9, either path eventually leads to terminal.  \n  - But from N2 → N0 → N7 or N8, it's a terminal move after that.  \n  - All branches from N3 eventually end in terminal moves.  \n- Moving to N4 leads to N5 (terminal) or N3 (same as above).  \n  - N5 is terminal, so that move ends the game immediately.  \n  - N3 leads into the same cycle as above.  \n\nThe key is that if you move to N4 and go to N5, the game ends immediately — opponent loses. So the move to N4 (to N5) ends the game immediately, meaning the player who moves to N4 and chooses N5 wins. That is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, choosing N4 and then going to N5 is a forced win.\n\nThus, the optimal choice from N6 is N4 (to force a terminal move via N5).</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6842954159592529, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N3 or N4.  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N9, then to N2 or N8.  \n  - N2 → N1 (terminal) or N0 → N7 or N8.  \n  - N0 → N7 (terminal) or N8 (terminal).  \n  - Thus, from N9, either path eventually leads to terminal.  \n  - But from N2 → N0 → N7 or N8, it's a terminal move after that.  \n  - All branches from N3 eventually end in terminal moves.  \n- Moving to N4 leads to N5 (terminal) or N3 (same as above).  \n  - N5 is terminal, so that move ends the game immediately.  \n  - N3 leads into the same cycle as above.  \n\nThe key is that if you move to N4 and go to N5, the game ends immediately — opponent loses. So the move to N4 (to N5) ends the game immediately, meaning the player who moves to N4 and chooses N5 wins. That is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, choosing N4 and then going to N5 is a forced win.\n\nThus, the optimal choice from N6 is N4 (to force a terminal move via N5).</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N9\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN4 -> N5, N3\nN9 -> N2, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N7, N8\n\nLegal moves: N5, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N3. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 0.49906621392190154, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N5 and N3. N5 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> N7, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN8 -> N13, N2\nN7 -> N4, N8\nN3 -> N10, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N3\nN11 -> N5, N10\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N0\nN4 -> N2, N11\n\nLegal moves: N7, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N7 or N3. Moving to N7 leads to N4 or N8; N4 leads to N2 (terminal) or N11 (to N5 or N10, both terminal), so N4 is a losing branch. N8 leads to N13 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). So both branches from N7 eventually end in terminal. From N3, the moves go to N10 (terminal) or N12. N12 leads to N7 or N6. N7 again leads to terminal paths. N6 leads to N9 or N0, both terminal. All paths from N1 eventually lead to terminal with no further options. Thus, both N7 and N3 are equivalent in outcome — no winning move exists. But since the player who cannot move loses, choosing any move transfers control to opponent. Therefore, both are equal, but we pick the one that avoids giving opponent a winning path. However, both N7 and N3 lead to symmetric terminal outcomes. Since no move gives a forced win, the best choice is N3, as it avoids immediate deep branching into N7/N8. But both are equal. However, N3 leads to N10 (immediate terminal), while N12 from N3 opens longer chains. But N12 leads to N7 or N6, which both lead to terminal. So no advantage. Ultimately, either move is equal. Choose N3 as it avoids a more complex path early.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7207979626485569, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N7 or N3. Moving to N7 leads to N4 or N8; N4 leads to N2 (terminal) or N11 (to N5 or N10, both terminal), so N4 is a losing branch. N8 leads to N13 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). So both branches from N7 eventually end in terminal. From N3, the moves go to N10 (terminal) or N12. N12 leads to N7 or N6. N7 again leads to terminal paths. N6 leads to N9 or N0, both terminal. All paths from N1 eventually lead to terminal with no further options. Thus, both N7 and N3 are equivalent in outcome — no winning move exists. But since the player who cannot move loses, choosing any move transfers control to opponent. Therefore, both are equal, but we pick the one that avoids giving opponent a winning path. However, both N7 and N3 lead to symmetric terminal outcomes. Since no move gives a forced win, the best choice is N3, as it avoids immediate deep branching into N7/N8. But both are equal. However, N3 leads to N10 (immediate terminal), while N12 from N3 opens longer chains. But N12 leads to N7 or N6, which both lead to terminal. So no advantage. Ultimately, either move is equal. Choose N3 as it avoids a more complex path early.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> N7, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN8 -> N13, N2\nN7 -> N4, N8\nN3 -> N10, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> N7, N3\nN11 -> N5, N10\nN13 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N0\nN4 -> N2, N11\n\nLegal moves: N10, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N10 and N12. N10 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N10 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4939728353140918, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N10 and N12. N10 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N10 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N9\nN0 -> N4, N7\nN6 -> N2\nN10 -> N5, N3\nN3 -> N11, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N10, N3\nN13 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N12, N1\nN7 -> N6\nN11 -> N8\nN12 -> N13\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. Evaluating both:  \n- N4 leads to N10 or N3. N10 leads to N5 or N3; N5 leads to N13 (terminal) or N9; N9 leads to N12 or N1; N1 leads to terminal. N3 leads to N11 or N7; N11 leads to N8 (terminal); N7 leads to N6 → N2 (terminal). All paths eventually terminate.  \n- N7 leads to N6 → N2 (terminal). This is a direct end.  \nBut in vertex geography, the player who cannot move loses. So, the player making a move that leads to an immediate terminal state (i.e., no further moves) is actually the one who wins — because the opponent has no move.  \nN7 → N6 → N2 (terminal): the chain ends immediately after one move. So, the player who moves to N7 forces the opponent to face a terminal state. This is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N7 leads to a win.  \nN4 leads to branching and more moves, and the game continues, possibly leading to longer chains. Since we want to maximize our final outcome (i.e., win), and N7 leads directly to a forced win (the opponent cannot move after), it is better.  \nTherefore, move to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.2953310696095075, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N7. Evaluating both:  \n- N4 leads to N10 or N3. N10 leads to N5 or N3; N5 leads to N13 (terminal) or N9; N9 leads to N12 or N1; N1 leads to terminal. N3 leads to N11 or N7; N11 leads to N8 (terminal); N7 leads to N6 → N2 (terminal). All paths eventually terminate.  \n- N7 leads to N6 → N2 (terminal). This is a direct end.  \nBut in vertex geography, the player who cannot move loses. So, the player making a move that leads to an immediate terminal state (i.e., no further moves) is actually the one who wins — because the opponent has no move.  \nN7 → N6 → N2 (terminal): the chain ends immediately after one move. So, the player who moves to N7 forces the opponent to face a terminal state. This is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N7 leads to a win.  \nN4 leads to branching and more moves, and the game continues, possibly leading to longer chains. Since we want to maximize our final outcome (i.e., win), and N7 leads directly to a forced win (the opponent cannot move after), it is better.  \nTherefore, move to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N9\nN0 -> N4, N7\nN6 -> N2\nN10 -> N5, N3\nN3 -> N11, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N10, N3\nN13 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N12, N1\nN7 -> N6\nN11 -> N8\nN12 -> N13\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N6. From N6, the only move is to N2 (terminal), which ends the game immediately. Thus, the player who moves to N6 (and then to N2) wins immediately, because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N6 is the only option and leads to an immediate win for the current player. No other choice exists.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.47699490662139216, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N6. From N6, the only move is to N2 (terminal), which ends the game immediately. Thus, the player who moves to N6 (and then to N2) wins immediately, because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N6 is the only option and leads to an immediate win for the current player. No other choice exists.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acycl