\nN3 -> N1, N0, N5\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.5016129032258064, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the only legal move is to N2. N2 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N0\nN8 -> N6\nN3 -> N8, N2\nN7 -> N3, N5\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N1\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N9\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N0. N7 leads to N3 or N5. N3 leads to N8 or N2 (N2 is terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately). N8 leads to N6 (terminal), so the opponent loses. N5 leads to N3 or N1. N1 leads to terminal. The path through N5 can loop back to N3, and eventually, the game may end with a win via forced move to terminal. However, N0 leads to N1 or N9. N1 and N9 both go to terminal — thus, the player moving from N0 forces the opponent into a terminal move and wins. Therefore, choosing N0 leads directly to a win (since the next player has to move from terminal, which is invalid). Hence, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6044991511035653, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N0. N7 leads to N3 or N5. N3 leads to N8 or N2 (N2 is terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately). N8 leads to N6 (terminal), so the opponent loses. N5 leads to N3 or N1. N1 leads to terminal. The path through N5 can loop back to N3, and eventually, the game may end with a win via forced move to terminal. However, N0 leads to N1 or N9. N1 and N9 both go to terminal — thus, the player moving from N0 forces the opponent into a terminal move and wins. Therefore, choosing N0 leads directly to a win (since the next player has to move from terminal, which is invalid). Hence, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N0\nN8 -> N6\nN3 -> N8, N2\nN7 -> N3, N5\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N1\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N9\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N1 and N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so moving to N9 ends the game immediately and wins. Thus, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.5067062818336163, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N1 and N9. N9 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so moving to N9 ends the game immediately and wins. Thus, choosing N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N2, N7\nN7 -> N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N3, N7\nN1 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N3, N2, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N3, N2, or N7. We analyze each:\n\n- N3 leads to N1 or N4. N1 goes to N8 (terminal), or N3 again (loop). N4 leads to terminal. So from N3, the opponent can force a win from symmetry or terminal paths.\n- N2 leads to N0. N0 leads to N6 (terminal), N3, or N7. This opens many paths; however, N3 and N7 continue the game. So it’s not terminal.\n- N7 leads to N9, which goes to terminal. So N7 ends the game immediately.\n\nIf you go to N7, the next player faces terminal — you win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N7 is optimal: it ends the game on the opponent's turn.\n\nTherefore, move to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.27201471420486706, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N3, N2, or N7. We analyze each:\n\n- N3 leads to N1 or N4. N1 goes to N8 (terminal), or N3 again (loop). N4 leads to terminal. So from N3, the opponent can force a win from symmetry or terminal paths.\n- N2 leads to N0. N0 leads to N6 (terminal), N3, or N7. This opens many paths; however, N3 and N7 continue the game. So it’s not terminal.\n- N7 leads to N9, which goes to terminal. So N7 ends the game immediately.\n\nIf you go to N7, the next player faces terminal — you win immediately.\n\nThus, choosing N7 is optimal: it ends the game on the opponent's turn.\n\nTherefore, move to N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N2, N7\nN7 -> N9\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N1, N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N3, N7\nN1 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N9. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. No other option exists, so this is the only choice.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.49906621392190154, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N9. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. No other option exists, so this is the only choice.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N2, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N8\nN4 -> N6, N5\nN0 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N0, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N0 or N4. Evaluating each:  \n- N0 leads to N2 or N1 (both terminal), so the next player loses. So moving to N0 wins immediately.  \n- N4 leads to N6 or N5.  \n  - N6 → N3 → N7 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), both terminal paths end with opponent's loss.  \n  - N5 → N2 or N6. N2 terminal, N6 continues.  \n    Eventually, paths from N4 can be followed, but due to depth and cycles, the game is won only if the opponent is forced to a terminal with no moves.  \nHowever, from N9, moving to N0 gives immediate terminal after one move (the opponent has no move from N2 or N1), so it ends the game immediately. That is a winning move. Thus, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.616383701188455, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N0 or N4. Evaluating each:  \n- N0 leads to N2 or N1 (both terminal), so the next player loses. So moving to N0 wins immediately.  \n- N4 leads to N6 or N5.  \n  - N6 → N3 → N7 (terminal) or N8 (terminal), both terminal paths end with opponent's loss.  \n  - N5 → N2 or N6. N2 terminal, N6 continues.  \n    Eventually, paths from N4 can be followed, but due to depth and cycles, the game is won only if the opponent is forced to a terminal with no moves.  \nHowever, from N9, moving to N0 gives immediate terminal after one move (the opponent has no move from N2 or N1), so it ends the game immediately. That is a winning move. Thus, N0 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N2, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N4\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N8\nN4 -> N6, N5\nN0 -> N2, N1\n\nLegal moves: N2, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N2 and N1. N2 and N1 both lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, either move results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, so either choice leads to victory. Choosing either is equivalent in outcome. However, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, any valid choice suffices. We select one such node.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4642614601018676, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N2 and N1. N2 and N1 both lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, either move results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, so either choice leads to victory. Choosing either is equivalent in outcome. However, since the goal is to maximize outcome and both lead to immediate win, any valid choice suffices. We select one such node.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N8, N1, N3\nN1 -> N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0, N5, N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N7\nN8 -> N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N1, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N3.  \n- N8 leads to N6, then to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately after the move. Thus, the current player wins only if they move to a node where the opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. So, current player loses.  \n- N3 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7. N9 is terminal, so if you go to N3, you can go to N7. From N7, options are N0 (terminal), N5 (terminal), or N1 (then to N0). All paths from N7 lead to terminal, so it's a losing position for the next player. Thus, from N3, the opponent is in a losing position. So going to N3 is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N3 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.2915393322014715, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N3.  \n- N8 leads to N6, then to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately after the move. Thus, the current player wins only if they move to a node where the opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- N1 leads to N0 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. So, current player loses.  \n- N3 leads to N9 (terminal) or N7. N9 is terminal, so if you go to N3, you can go to N7. From N7, options are N0 (terminal), N5 (terminal), or N1 (then to N0). All paths from N7 lead to terminal, so it's a losing position for the next player. Thus, from N3, the opponent is in a losing position. So going to N3 is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N3 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: 