ame immediately and wins. Thus, choosing N11 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3\nN5 -> N1, N7, N2\nN7 -> N6\nN4 -> N1, N7, N5\nN6 -> N8, N9\n\nLegal moves: N1, N7, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N1, N7, or N5. We analyze each:  \n- N1 leads to N3 (terminal), so opponent wins immediately. This is a losing move.  \n- N7 leads to N6, which can go to N8 or N9. N8 and N9 are terminal, so no further play. The player to move from N6 (i.e., after reaching N7) can choose terminal. So N7 is followed by a win.  \n- N5 leads to N1 (N3), N7 (N6), or N2 (terminal). N2 is terminal, so player can win. From here, N5 branches to a winning move, possibly forcing opponent into terminal.  \n\nIn a game like this, the first player can choose from N5 to win directly by forcing opponent into a terminal path. Thus, N5 leads to control of a winning move.  \nWe maximize our chance of winning by choosing a node with a winning path: N5 leads to at least one path where we win.  \nThus, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.3136106395019808, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N1, N7, or N5. We analyze each:  \n- N1 leads to N3 (terminal), so opponent wins immediately. This is a losing move.  \n- N7 leads to N6, which can go to N8 or N9. N8 and N9 are terminal, so no further play. The player to move from N6 (i.e., after reaching N7) can choose terminal. So N7 is followed by a win.  \n- N5 leads to N1 (N3), N7 (N6), or N2 (terminal). N2 is terminal, so player can win. From here, N5 branches to a winning move, possibly forcing opponent into terminal.  \n\nIn a game like this, the first player can choose from N5 to win directly by forcing opponent into a terminal path. Thus, N5 leads to control of a winning move.  \nWe maximize our chance of winning by choosing a node with a winning path: N5 leads to at least one path where we win.  \nThus, N5 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3\nN5 -> N1, N7, N2\nN7 -> N6\nN4 -> N1, N7, N5\nN6 -> N8, N9\n\nLegal moves: N1, N7, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1, N7, or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.1623372948500283, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1, N7, or N2. N2 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> N12, N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N10\nN6 -> N0\nN12 -> N2, N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN13 -> N11\nN8 -> N6, N5\nN7 -> N4\nN10 -> N11, N13\n\nLegal moves: N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N3 and N10. Analyzing both:  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N12 or N2. N12 goes to N2 or N7, and N7 leads to N4 to N9 (terminal). N2 can lead into N8 or N7, eventually leading to N6 or N5 or N0 (terminal). This path opens a loop or a terminal depending on depth and symmetry.  \n- N10 leads to N11 (terminal) or N13 to N11. Both end the game.  \n\nFrom a game theory perspective, a player forcing a terminal on the opponent’s turn wins. N10 ends the game directly. The opponent cannot move after this. Thus, moving to N10 is a winning move — the next player will lose immediately. So, choosing N10 is optimal. Therefore, we pick the move that ends the game with certainty.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6214770797962649, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N3 and N10. Analyzing both:  \n- Moving to N3 leads to N12 or N2. N12 goes to N2 or N7, and N7 leads to N4 to N9 (terminal). N2 can lead into N8 or N7, eventually leading to N6 or N5 or N0 (terminal). This path opens a loop or a terminal depending on depth and symmetry.  \n- N10 leads to N11 (terminal) or N13 to N11. Both end the game.  \n\nFrom a game theory perspective, a player forcing a terminal on the opponent’s turn wins. N10 ends the game directly. The opponent cannot move after this. Thus, moving to N10 is a winning move — the next player will lose immediately. So, choosing N10 is optimal. Therefore, we pick the move that ends the game with certainty.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> N12, N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N10\nN6 -> N0\nN12 -> N2, N7\nN5 -> terminal\nN13 -> N11\nN8 -> N6, N5\nN7 -> N4\nN10 -> N11, N13\n\nLegal moves: N11, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N11 and N13. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4931239388794566, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N11 and N13. N11 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> te