[2026-08-12 13:54:32] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN6 -> N2, N1\nN2 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4\nN1 -> N3, N9\n\nLegal moves: N6, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6 and N7. Analyzing each move:  \n- Move to N6: leads to N2 or N1. N2 goes to terminal or N3 (N3 leads to terminal). N1 leads to N3 (terminal) or N9 (N4 terminal). All paths end in terminal, thus no immediate loss.  \n- Move to N7: leads to N2 (then N8 or N3), both terminal, or N0 (terminal). All are terminal. But crucially, the player who moves to N7 (to a node with only terminal paths) leaves opponent in a terminal state, so this is a losing state for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N7 is better because opponent has no legal moves after it.  \nThus, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.5977079796264856, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N6 and N7. Analyzing each move:  \n- Move to N6: leads to N2 or N1. N2 goes to terminal or N3 (N3 leads to terminal). N1 leads to N3 (terminal) or N9 (N4 terminal). All paths end in terminal, thus no immediate loss.  \n- Move to N7: leads to N2 (then N8 or N3), both terminal, or N0 (terminal). All are terminal. But crucially, the player who moves to N7 (to a node with only terminal paths) leaves opponent in a terminal state, so this is a losing state for the opponent. Therefore, choosing N7 is better because opponent has no legal moves after it.  \nThus, the best choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N6, N7\nN6 -> N2, N1\nN2 -> N8, N3\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4\nN1 -> N3, N9\n\nLegal moves: N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2 or N0. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N7, N2, N9\nN2 -> N1, N8, N7\nN7 -> N1\nN5 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N0\nN9 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N2, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7, N2, or N9. We analyze each destination:  \n- N7 leads to N1, then to N5 or N0. From N5 to N6 (terminal), or N0 (terminal). Player who faces terminal after move wins, so player to move wins if opponent is forced into terminal. Thus, if reach terminal from next player, it's a win. So, all paths from N7 end up in a forced win or loss depending on turn order.  \n- N2 → leads to N1 (N5, N0) or N8 → terminal or N7 again (loop with N1). From N2 to N8 leads directly to terminal. So it's possible to force opponent to lose by moving to N8.  \n- N9 → to N3 → terminal. Direct terminal. This gives immediate end on opponent's move after this, meaning current player to move can control it. So this ends the game in a win if player moves directly.  \n\nBest outcome: Move to N9 to end game immediately, with opponent with no legal move next. This is terminal, so it's a win.  \nTherefore, move to N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.33398415393322023, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7, N2, or N9. We analyze each destination:  \n- N7 leads to N1, then to N5 or N0. From N5 to N6 (terminal), or N0 (terminal). Player who faces terminal after move wins, so player to move wins if opponent is forced into terminal. Thus, if reach terminal from next player, it's a win. So, all paths from N7 end up in a forced win or loss depending on turn order.  \n- N2 → leads to N1 (N5, N0) or N8 → terminal or N7 again (loop with N1). From N2 to N8 leads directly to terminal. So it's possible to force opponent to lose by moving to N8.  \n- N9 → to N3 → terminal. Direct terminal. This gives immediate end on opponent's move after this, meaning current player to move can control it. So this ends the game in a win if player moves directly.  \n\nBest outcome: Move to N9 to end game immediately, with opponent with no legal move next. This is terminal, so it's a win.  \nTherefore, move to N9 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N7, N2, N9\nN2 -> N1, N8, N7\nN7 -> N1\nN5 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N5, N0\nN9 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.5016129032258064, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N3. N3 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> N8, N5\nN2 -> N7\nN8 -> N3, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N6 or N5. Evaluating each:\n- Move to N5: Then from N5, we can go to N1 (win for opponent) or N2. N2 leads to N7 (terminal, so the next player loses). N1 leads to terminal directly. So any choice from N5 ends in a win for the next player. Thus, the opponent will win.\n- Move to N6: From N6 we can go to N8 or N5. \n   - N8 leads to N3 (to N9 or N4, both terminal), or N1 (terminal). So from N8 either path ends the game with a terminal, meaning opponent has no choice, and we win.\n   - N5 is again a losing path as shown above.\n\nTherefore, going to N6 leads to control of terminal states and the ability to force the opponent into a losing position. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3785229202037352, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N6 or N5. Evaluating each:\n- Move to N5: Then from N5, we can go to N1 (win for opponent) or N2. N2 leads to N7 (terminal, so the next player loses). N1 leads to terminal directly. So any choice from N5 ends in a win for the next player. Thus, the opponent will win.\n- Move to N6: From N6 we can go to N8 or N5. \n   - N8 leads to N3 (to N9 or N4, both terminal), or N1 (terminal). So from N8 either path ends the game with a terminal, meaning opponent has no choice, and we win.\n   - N5 is again a losing path as shown above.\n\nTherefore, going to N6 leads to control of terminal states and the ability to force the opponent into a losing position. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> N8, N5\nN2 -> N7\nN8 -> N3, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N8 and N5. N5 leads to N1 or N2. N2 leads to N7 (terminal), and N1 is terminal, so all paths from N5 end in terminal. If player moves to N5, the next player faces terminal or a terminal path. N8 leads to N3 or N1. N3 leads to N9 or N4, both terminal, and N1 is terminal. Thus, both paths from N8 and N5 lead to terminal in next turn or immediate loss to opponent. But N8 gives more active branching and options to force opponent into terminal. Hence, move to N8 is optimal because it maintains game flow and avoids early loss. Thus, choosing N8 maximizes chance of win.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4116298811544991, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N8 and N5. N5 leads to N1 or N2. N2 leads to N7 (terminal), and N1 is terminal, so all paths from N5 end in terminal. If player moves to N5, the next player faces terminal or a terminal path. N8 leads to N3 or N1. N3 leads to N9 or N4, both terminal, and N1 is terminal. Thus, both paths from N8 and N5 lead to terminal in next turn or immediate loss to opponent. But N8 gives more active branching and options to force opponent into terminal. Hence, move to N8 is optimal because it maintains game flow and avoids early loss. Thus, choosing N8 maximizes chance of win.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N1, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N6, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> N8, N5\nN2 -> N7\nN8 -> N3, N1\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N3 and N1. N1 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N1 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N3 and N1. N1 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N1 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N9\nN4 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N2, N8\nN5 -> N1, N4\nN9 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N9, N2, or N8. We analyze each move to see which leads to a winning position (assuming optimal play from both players). \n\n- Move to N9: Then at N9, opponent can go to N5 or N7.\n    - If to N5 → leads to N1/N4 → N1 ends in terminal (loss for next player), N4 → N6 → terminal.\n    - From N5, the chain leads into either terminal, and the player after reaching terminal is forced to lose.\n    - Ultimately N9 leads to a sequence that may result in the opponent winning under correct play.\n- Move to N2 → N2 to N3 or N7; both end in terminal. So from N2:\n    - N3 → terminal, next player loses. So this move is terminal, and the next player has no valid path. So this move may be favorable.\n- Move to N8 → N8 to N9, which then as above continues.\n\nNow, N2 → to terminal (either directly or by N3 or N7) — all lead to end. But terminal after a move means the opponent cannot move and hence the current player has won.\n\nSo the idea: after visiting N2, all paths go directly into terminal — thus the opponent has no valid move after.\n\nThis is a winning strategy. So choosing N2 gives immediate terminal, the next player cannot move — the first player (current) wins.\n\nThus, the best move is to N2.\n\nTherefore, choosing move N2 maximizes the chance to win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.3993491794001133, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N9, N2, or N8. We analyze each move to see which leads to a winning position (assuming optimal play from both players). \n\n- Move to N9: Then at N9, opponent can go to N5 or N7.\n    - If to N5 → leads to N1/N4 → N1 ends in terminal (loss for next player), N4 → N6 → terminal.\n    - From N5, the chain leads into either terminal, and the player after reaching terminal is forced to lose.\n    - Ultimately N9 leads to a sequence that may result in the opponent winning under correct play.\n- Move to N2 → N2 to N3 or N7; both end in terminal. So from N2:\n    - N3 → terminal, next player loses. So this move is terminal, and the next player has no valid path. So this move may be favorable.\n- Move to N8 → N8 to N9, which then as above continues.\n\nNow, N2 → to terminal (either directly or by N3 or N7) — all lead to end. But terminal after a move means the opponent cannot move and hence the current player has won.\n\nSo the idea: after visiting N2, all paths go directly into terminal — thus the opponent has no valid move after.\n\nThis is a winning strategy. So choosing N2 gives immediate terminal, the next player cannot move — the first player (current) wins.\n\nThus, the best move is to N2.\n\nTherefore, choosing move N2 maximizes the chance to win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N9\nN4 -> N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N2, N8\nN5 -> N1, N4\nN9 -> N5, N7\nN2 -> N3, N7\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.49906621392190154, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads directly to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move and loses. Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N13, N12\nN11 -> N1, N4, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3\nN13 -> terminal\nN4 -> N13, N10\nN3 -> terminal\nN10 -> N2, N7, N9, N6\nN0 -> N8\nN12 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N1, N4, and N6. We analyze each choice:  \n- To N1: N1 → N4 → options from N4: N13 (win), N10 → leads to complex branching but N10 → N6 (back to N6 → N5 → N3 terminal) or N2, N7 (terminal). Eventually leads to player to lose if opponent plays optimally.  \n- To N4: From N4, can go to N13 (terminal) or N10. N10 leads to N2, N7 (terminal), N9, or N6 again. N6 leads to N5 → N3 terminal. But N9 → N0 → N8 → terminal; it's a loop with possible wins. This path is volatile.  \n- To N6: Then go to N5 → N3 terminal (immediate win). So from N11 → N6 → N5 → N3. No further options after. This is a direct and forced win (i.e., opponent has no next move). Thus, moving to N6 forces a win. Therefore, choosing N6 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.0001980758347482, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N1, N4, and N6. We analyze each choice:  \n- To N1: N1 → N4 → options from N4: N13 (win), N10 → leads to complex branching but N10 → N6 (back to N6 → N5 → N3 terminal) or N2, N7 (terminal). Eventually leads to player to lose if opponent plays optimally.  \n- To N4: From N4, can go to N13 (terminal) or N10. N10 leads to N2, N7 (terminal), N9, or N6 again. N6 leads to N5 → N3 terminal. But N9 → N0 → N8 → terminal; it's a loop with possible wins. This path is volatile.  \n- To N6: Then go to N5 → N3 terminal (immediate win). So from N11 → N6 → N5 → N3. No further options after. This is a direct and forced win (i.e., opponent has no next move). Thus, moving to N6 forces a win. Therefore, choosing N6 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
