inal → immediate win (opponent has no choice).  \n    - Thus, from N12 → N4 → N3 is winning (direct terminal).  \nThus, choosing N4 gives immediate terminal at N3 and a win.  \nN4 is better.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6172325976230899, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N4 or N7.  \n- Moving to N7: Then to N8, leading to N13 or N9.  \n    - From N13 → N4/N5, both eventually lead to terminal or loops.  \n    - N9 → N2 → terminal or N1 → N0/N16 → both N16 and N0 (N0 → N6/N11 → terminal), so all terminal.  \n    So N7 leads to a forced win for opponent, and no winning path from N7.\n- Moving to N4: Next options N14/N3.  \n    - N3 → terminal → immediate win (opponent has no choice).  \n    - Thus, from N12 → N4 → N3 is winning (direct terminal).  \nThus, choosing N4 gives immediate terminal at N3 and a win.  \nN4 is better.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N2, N1\nN1 -> N0, N16\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN10 -> N15\nN7 -> N8\nN12 -> N4, N7\nN5 -> N10, N17\nN8 -> N13, N9\nN17 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N14, N3\nN0 -> N6, N11\nN13 -> N4, N5\nN14 -> N11, N15\nN16 -> terminal\nN15 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N14, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N14 and N3. N3 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4982173174872666, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N14 and N3. N3 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N3, N7, N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N1\nN13 -> N5, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N7, N6, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN11 -> N2\nN10 -> N11\nN4 -> terminal\nN12 -> N13, N10, N0\n\nLegal moves: N13, N10, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N13, N10, or N0. We analyze each:\n- N0 leads to edges to N3 (terminal), N7 (terminal), or N8 (then to N4, terminal). All terminal: immediate loss if opponent plays next; thus N0 is losing for the next player.\n- N10 leads to N11, then to N2, which is terminal. After N13, then to N5 or N2, N2 is terminal; if N5 is taken from there, it leads to branching, but N5 leads to N7 and N6 which go to N9 (terminal) or N0 (terminal), both losing. So N13 leads to terminal paths with minimal options, meaning a win via N13.\n- N13 leads directly to N5 (branching: N7, N6, N0), N2 (terminal). From N2, all paths are terminal. N6 leads to N9, N1, both terminal. All routes are dead ends after N13 or N10.\nN13 offers more control over branching and can force win if used properly, due to terminal chains. Hence, move to N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.3433220147142049, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N13, N10, or N0. We analyze each:\n- N0 leads to edges to N3 (terminal), N7 (terminal), or N8 (then to N4, terminal). All terminal: immediate loss if opponent plays next; thus N0 is losing for the next player.\n- N10 leads to N11, then to N2, which is terminal. After N13, then to N5 or N2, N2 is terminal; if N5 is taken from there, it leads to branching, but N5 leads to N7 and N6 which go to N9 (terminal) or N0 (terminal), both losing. So N13 leads to terminal paths with minimal options, meaning a win via N13.\n- N13 leads directly to N5 (branching: N7, N6, N0), N2 (terminal). From N2, all paths are terminal. N6 leads to N9, N1, both terminal. All routes are dead ends after N13 or N10.\nN13 offers more control over branching and can force win if used properly, due to terminal chains. Hence, move to N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}]
