, N6, or N7.  \n- N6 leads to N0, which leads to terminal → the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). So, moving to N6 ends the game immediately on the opponent's turn, resulting in a win.  \n- N5: Then the opponent can move to N1 (winning), or N9 (leading to N8 → N4, then terminal). Either path eventually leads to opponent’s advantage.  \n- N7 → N9 → N8 → N4 or N9 → N2; either path leads to terminal eventually, but with a sequence that may be exploitable. Still, both N5 and N7 leave more active play for the opponent.  \nThus, N6 gives a direct win by forcing immediate end of game. N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.274561403508772, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N5, N6, or N7.  \n- N6 leads to N0, which leads to terminal → the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). So, moving to N6 ends the game immediately on the opponent's turn, resulting in a win.  \n- N5: Then the opponent can move to N1 (winning), or N9 (leading to N8 → N4, then terminal). Either path eventually leads to opponent’s advantage.  \n- N7 → N9 → N8 → N4 or N9 → N2; either path leads to terminal eventually, but with a sequence that may be exploitable. Still, both N5 and N7 leave more active play for the opponent.  \nThus, N6 gives a direct win by forcing immediate end of game. N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> termin