ally all such branches lead to terminal.  \n\nBut key insight: from N10 → N2 leads to immediate win as it ends game (N13 or N4 terminal), opponent cannot move. N9 → N3 → N0 → N5 → N6 → terminal is possible. All paths from N9 or N2 eventually end with terminal nodes due to terminal conditions in the graph. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome — i.e., force win — N10 → N2 ends the game immediately, leaving opponent with no legal play. Hence, choosing N2 is best to guarantee win.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6622241086587437, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N2 and N9.  \n- Moving to N2: Path goes to N13 or N4, both terminal. So player reaches terminal after one move; opponent has no legal move. This leads to a win for the current player.  \n- Moving to N9: Then N9 → N1, then N1 → N7 → N12 or N3 → N11 or N8 → N11/N5, eventually leading to loops or dead ends depending on the path, but eventually all such branches lead to terminal.  \n\nBut key insight: from N10 → N2 leads to immediate win as it ends game (N13 or N4 terminal), opponent cannot move. N9 → N3 → N0 → N5 → N6 → terminal is possible. All paths from N9 or N2 eventually end with terminal nodes due to terminal conditions in the graph. Since the goal is to maximize final game outcome — i.e., force win — N10 → N2 ends the game immediately, leaving opponent with no legal play. Hence, choosing N2 is best to guarantee win.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format res