[2026-08-12 14:06:11] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN1 -> N8, N7\nN5 -> N6\nN7 -> N5, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N1\n\nLegal moves: N5, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N5 or N1. Evaluating each choice:  \n- Move to N5: Then to N6 (terminal), so the opponent has no move from N6 (end of game). This is a winning move for the current player.  \n- Move to N1: From N1 leads to either N8 or N7 (both lead into cycles or terminal). Further analysis shows:  \n   - Path through N8 to N4 or N2: leads to terminal via N2 → N3 or N0, all terminal paths.  \n   - Path from N1 → N8 → N4 (or N2) → N3/N0 — all terminal, and the game ends. But the key is whether the opponent has options.  \n   The path N1 → N7 → N4 (terminal), also ends. Thus, all branches from N1 eventually lead into terminal states or loop into losing positions.  \nThus, choosing N5 gives immediate terminal win for current player, while N1 gives opponent better chance.  \nTherefore, move to N5 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6469439728353141, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N5 or N1. Evaluating each choice:  \n- Move to N5: Then to N6 (terminal), so the opponent has no move from N6 (end of game). This is a winning move for the current player.  \n- Move to N1: From N1 leads to either N8 or N7 (both lead into cycles or terminal). Further analysis shows:  \n   - Path through N8 to N4 or N2: leads to terminal via N2 → N3 or N0, all terminal paths.  \n   - Path from N1 → N8 → N4 (or N2) → N3/N0 — all terminal, and the game ends. But the key is whether the opponent has options.  \n   The path N1 → N7 → N4 (terminal), also ends. Thus, all branches from N1 eventually lead into terminal states or loop into losing positions.  \nThus, choosing N5 gives immediate terminal win for current player, while N1 gives opponent better chance.  \nTherefore, move to N5 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN2 -> N3, N0\nN1 -> N8, N7\nN5 -> N6\nN7 -> N5, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N1\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the only legal move is to N6, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the only legal move is to N6, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> N9, N13\nN7 -> N0, N6, N3\nN13 -> N11\nN4 -> N2, N0\nN2 -> N1\nN10 -> N12, N3, N4\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N8, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN12 -> N7, N5\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N12, N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N12, N3, or N4. We analyze the outcomes of each:  \n- Move to N3: From N3, paths go to N9 or N13. N9 ends at terminal, and N13 leads to N11 (terminal). Thus, opponent wins on both paths (we lose).  \n- Move to N4: N4 → N2 → N1 (terminal), opponent wins. No path avoids immediate terminal, so losing.  \n- Move to N12: Then N12 → N7 → N6 or N0 (both end terminal). All routes eventually lead to opponent winning, so also losing.  \n\nAll moves lead to losing positions. But the goal is to maximize outcome. Since all are losing, no win is possible, and the game ends immediately with no move left on the opponent’s turn. However, since there is no legal win path in any choice, we pick the move with best chance to avoid immediate loss — all are equivalent. Selecting one, N3 is reachable directly from N10, and it is in a subgraph where all paths are terminal. Thus, the choice of N3 is valid and optimal among equal options.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.3365308432371252, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N12, N3, or N4. We analyze the outcomes of each:  \n- Move to N3: From N3, paths go to N9 or N13. N9 ends at terminal, and N13 leads to N11 (terminal). Thus, opponent wins