t is best.  \nWe choose to go from N13 to N11 to maximize our win chance, forcing the opponent to face losing options.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.5462082625919639, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N4, N0, or N11. We analyze each destination:  \n- Move to N4: Then opponent faces N4. N4 leads to N2 (terminal), N0, or N2 from a choice. N0 is recursive and leads to N3, etc. N13→N4→N0 leads to N3 or N11, both leading back to N12→N5→terminal, or N13→N0→N3→N11→N6 or N0→N12→N5, so we track depth: from N4 to N2 directly. N4 leads via loop or to terminal (e.g., N4→N2). This is short-term win.  \n- Move to N0: From N0: can go to N12 (→N5) or N3 (→N11 or N8 → terminal). If to N12 leads to N5 (lose), N11 leads to N6 (lose) or N10 leads to N2, so terminal. Thus, N13→N4→N0→N3→N11→N6 → lose?  \n- Move to N11: N13→N11→N10→N2 (terminal), N6 (terminal). So N13→N11→N10 leads to N2 → terminal (opponent has no choice). So N13→N11 leads to opponent at N10 with move to terminal. N13→N11→N6 or →N11→N11 is terminal? No. But N10→N2 is terminal. So, if you go N4 or N0 to terminal chains, N13→N11 is better because from N11, player must go to either terminal. So N13→N11 ends in terminal path.\n\nThus, going to N11 (next state to N10 and to N11’s edges) ends in forced terminal for next player, and N11 has no way to continue. Thus N13 → N10 → terminal path. So it is best.  \nWe choose to go from N13 to N11 to maximize our win chance, forcing the opponent to face losing options.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format re