layers alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N6, N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN17 -> terminal\nN10 -> N13, N7\nN3 -> N12, N1\nN5 -> N17, N9\nN11 -> N14\nN1 -> N16\nN8 -> N3, N14\nN0 -> N15, N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN16 -> terminal\nN6 -> N10, N0\nN15 -> N11\nN7 -> N4, N8\nN13 -> N5\nN14 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6 or N3. We analyze both:  \n- Move to N6: then from N6 we can go to N10 or N0.  \n   - Path N6 → N10 → N13 → N5 → N17: N17 is terminal, thus the opponent wins.  \n   - N6 → N10 → N7 → N8 → N3 → N12 → terminal, all paths eventually lead to terminal.  \n   - N6 → N10 → N10 → N10 → N0 → N15 → N11 → N14 → terminal: all lead to terminal.  \n   Thus, from N6, the opponent can always reach terminal eventually, meaning the player who starts from N6 will lose due to no legal move after opponent's turns.  \n- Move to N3: from N3, options to N12 or N1.  \n   - Path N3 → N1 → N16 → terminal.  \n   - N3 → N12 → terminal.  \n   All these result in the opponent’s winning.  \nThus, no matter which choice, all options result in opponent winning.  \nThus, the player at N2, by moving to either destination, leads to opponent being able to finish the game. Since no further legal move is available in a loop and all paths terminate to terminal, the player with no move loses.  \nThe best choice (to maximize final outcome) is a move leading to opponent’s inevitable loss, but in such a case both choices (N3, N6) seem to allow opponent win. However, since we must maximize the final game outcome, we pick N3 due to potential for better terminal control. But actually, all lead terminal. Therefore, both are equivalent.  \nBut: N3 has an edge to N1 → N16, which is terminal. N6 leads via to N0 → N15 → N11 → N14 → terminal, etc.  \nThus both are safe for terminal reach. But in DAVG, no move leads to a loop to preserve a win.  \nTherefore, both are losing positions — the player who makes a move in such structure ends up in a state where they can't stop the opponent's win.  \nHence, both are equal.  \nHowever, choosing one is required.  \nWe pick N3 — more options from it.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.885483870967742, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6 or N3. We analyze both:  \n- Move to N6: then from N6 we can go to N10 or N0.  \n   - Path N6 → N10 → N13 → N5 → N17: N17 is terminal, thus the opponent wins.  \n   - N6 → N10 → N7 → N8 → N3 → N12 → terminal, all paths eventually lead to terminal.  \n   - N6 → N10 → N10 → N10 → N0 → N15 → N11 → N14 → terminal: all lead to terminal.  \n   Thus, from N6, the opponent can always reach terminal eventually, meaning the player who starts from N6 will lose due to no legal move after opponent's turns.  \n- Move to N3: from N3, options to N12 or N1.  \n   - Path N3 → N1 → N16 → terminal.  \n   - N3 → N12 → terminal.  \n   All these result in the opponent’s winning.  \nThus, no matter which choice, all options result in opponent winning.  \nThus, the player at N2, by moving to either destination, leads to opponent being able to finish the game. Since no further legal move is available in a loop and all paths terminate to terminal, the player with no move loses.  \nThe best choice (to maximize final outcome) is a move leading to opponent’s inevitable loss, but in such a case both choices (N3, N6) seem to allow opponent win. However, since we must maximize the final game outcome, we pick N3 due to potential for better terminal control. But actually, all lead terminal. Therefore, both are equivalent.  \nBut: N3 has an edge to N1 → N16, which is terminal. N6 leads via to N0 → N15 → N11 → N14 → terminal, etc.  \nThus both are safe for terminal reach. But in DAVG, no move leads to a loop to preserve a win.  \nTherefore, both are losing positions — the player who makes a move in such structure ends up in a state where they can't stop the opponent's win.  \nHence, both are equal.  \nHowever, choosing one is required.  \nWe pick N3 — more options from it.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N6, N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN17 -> terminal\nN10 -> N13, N7\nN3 -> N12, N1\nN5 -> N17, N9\nN11 -> N14\nN1 -> N16\nN8 -> N3, N14\nN0 -> N15, N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN16 -> terminal\nN6 -> N10, N0\nN15 -> N11\nN7 -> N4, N8\nN13 -> N5\nN14 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N12, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N12 and N1. N12 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N12 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N12 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4948217317487267, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N12 and N1. N12 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N12 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N12 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N12.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one direc