ly.  \n- N7 → N4: leads to N4 → N2 or N4 → N11. N2 or N11 (via N11 → N3, N1 → terminal etc.) results in eventual terminal states. But N4 → N11 is a strong fork.\n- N7 → N8 → N10 (terminal): the opponent cannot move from N10 → so they lose → this is a win.\n- N7 → N13 → N6 → N3 → N12 → N1 → terminal, so opponent wins by now.\nN7 → N4: can reach N11 → N13 → N6 → N3 → N12 → N1 → terminal, or to N0 or N12 → terminal. Long chains eventually go to terminal. But N11 → N8 → N10 → terminal: both N10 and N1 end terminals.\nN7 → N8 leads directly to terminal via N8 → N10 → terminal. The opponent will be in terminal on their turn after move from N10 — thus, they have no move — they lose. This is a win.\nThus, N8 leads to a forced win immediately. N13 and N4 allow opponent to move to terminal paths that might avoid immediate loss. However, N8 ends the game instantly after one move.\nTherefore, choosing N8 guarantees a win — the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.40868704018109797, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4, N8, or N13. We analyze each option via game theory: we seek a move that forces a win or at least avoids losing immediately.  \n- N7 → N4: leads to N4 → N2 or N4 → N11. N2 or N11 (via N11 → N3, N1 → terminal etc.) results in eventual terminal states. But N4 → N11 is a strong fork.\n- N7 → N8 → N10 (terminal): the opponent cannot move from N10 → so they lose → this is a win.\n- N7 → N13 → N6 → N3 → N12 → N1 → terminal, so opponent wins by now.\nN7 → N4: can reach N11 → N13 → N6 → N3 → N12 → N1 → terminal, or to N0 or N12 → terminal. Long chains eventually go to terminal. But N11 → N8 → N10 → terminal: both N10 and N1 end terminals.\nN7 → N8 leads directly to terminal via N8 → N10 → terminal. The opponent will be in terminal on their turn after move from N10 — thus, they have no move — they lose. This is a win.\nThus, N8 leads to a forced win immediately. N13 and N4 allow opponent to move to terminal paths that might avoid immediate loss. However, N8 ends the game instantly after one move.\nTherefore, choosing N8 guarantees a win — the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N6\nN6 -> N3\nN9 -> N0, N5, N12\nN12 -> N1\nN3 -> N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N10\nN4 -> N2, N11\nN10 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N8, N13\nN1 -> terminal\nN11 -> N13, N9, N6\nN0 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the only legal move is to N10. N10 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N10 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N10 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4897283531409168, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the only legal move is to N10. N10 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N10 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N10 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N5, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N9, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N9\nN8 -> N6\nN5 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N1, N9, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N5. We analyze each destination to determine the optimal move that maximizes the chance of winning.  \n- Move to N1: Then the next state is N1 → N5 or N4. From N1, next player can go to N5 or N4 (N5→N8, N4→N2/N9). Eventually, all paths feed to terminal or cycle through states like N3/N7/N2 which terminate (terminal). This is not favorable for immediate win.  \n- Move to N5: N5→N8 → N6 → terminal. So the next player is at terminal and loses → current player wins. This is a direct win.  \n- Move to N9: N9→N3 or N7, both terminals (current player wins immediately if opponent cannot continue). So both paths terminate — also a win.\n\nThus, moves to N5 or N9 both lead to a win. But N5 leads to a single path to terminal after one move. N9 splits — one branch leads to terminal, other goes to N4 → N2 or N9, creating potential loops or longer chains.  \n\nBut N1 → N5 leads to N8 → N6 terminal — win for the player who moves from N5 onward.\n\nHowever, the key insight: from N0 to N9 allows control over the game flow and possibly longer chains, but critical observation is N1 and N4 have recursive paths. The most decisive move is to N5: the next player is forced to move from N5 to N8 to N6 → terminal, thus losing on their turn.\n\nN9 allows branching, which may offer more complexity and possibly counter-play. But since N0 → N5 → N8 → N6 → terminal is direct, and the opponent has no move after that, it's an immediate win. Thus, N5 ensures a direct win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N5 guarantees a win with no risk.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.5293435200905487, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N1, N9, or N5. We analyze each destination to determine the optimal move that maximizes the chance of winning.  \n- Move to N1: Then the next state is N1 → N5 or N4. From N1, next player can go to N5 or N4 (N5→N8, N4→N2/N9). Eventually, all paths feed to terminal or cycle through states like N3/N7/N2 which terminate (terminal). This is not favorable for immediate win.  \n- Move to N5: N5→N8 → N6 → terminal. So the next player is at terminal and loses → current player wins. This is a direct win.  \n- Move to N9: N9→N3 or N7, both terminals (current player wins immediately if opponent cannot continue). So both paths terminate — also a win.\n\nThus, moves to N5 or N9 both lead to a win. But N5 leads to a single path to terminal after one move. N9 splits — one branch leads to terminal, other goes to N4 → N2 or N9, creating potential loops or longer chains.  \n\nBut N1 → N5 leads to N8 → N6 terminal — win for the player who moves from N5 onward.\n\nHowever, the key insight: from N0 to N9 allows control over the game flow and possibly longer chains, but critical observation is N1 and N4 have recursive paths. The most decisive move is to N5: the next player is forced to move from N5 to N8 to N6 → terminal, thus losing on their turn.\n\nN9 allows branching, which may offer more complexity and possibly counter-play. But since N0 → N5 → N8 → N6 → terminal is direct, and the opponent has no move after that, it's an immediate win. Thus, N5 ensures a direct win.\n\nTherefore, choosing N5 guarantees a win with no risk.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
