[2026-08-12 14:19:22] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N7, N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN6 -> N11, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N12, N6\nN0 -> N10, N11\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN13 -> N1, N9\nN1 -> N0, N5\nN8 -> N13, N4\nN12 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N13 or N4.  \nFrom N13: next moves to N1 or N9  \nFrom N9 → N3 (terminal) or N5 (terminal)  \nN1 → N0 → N10 or N11 → terminal  \nN0 → terminal  \nSo N1 → N0 → N11 → terminal or N0 → N10 → terminal  \nBut from N2 → N12 → terminal or N6 → N11 → terminal  \nN → N7 → terminal  \nBack to key paths: N8 → N4 → N7 → terminal (immediate win)  \nN8 → N13 → N9 → N3 or N5 (both terminal), or → N1 → N0 → terminal.  \nAll paths from N13 and N1 end at terminals eventually unless the opponent is trapped earlier.  \nThus, from N8 → N4 → N7 (terminal) wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \nTherefore, N4 leads to an immediate win, while N13 leads to a longer path where opponent plays on the graph until terminal, possibly leading to draw or loss.  \nMaximize outcome: move to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6698641765704585, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N13 or N4.  \nFrom N13: next moves to N1 or N9  \nFrom N9 → N3 (terminal) or N5 (terminal)  \nN1 → N0 → N10 or N11 → terminal  \nN0 → terminal  \nSo N1 → N0 → N11 → terminal or N0 → N10 → terminal  \nBut from N2 → N12 → terminal or N6 → N11 → terminal  \nN → N7 → terminal  \nBack to key paths: N8 → N4 → N7 → terminal (immediate win)  \nN8 → N13 → N9 → N3 or N5 (both terminal), or → N1 → N0 → terminal.  \nAll paths from N13 and N1 end at terminals eventually unless the opponent is trapped earlier.  \nThus, from N8 → N4 → N7 (terminal) wins immediately (opponent has no move).  \nTherefore, N4 leads to an immediate win, while N13 leads to a longer path where opponent plays on the graph until terminal, possibly leading to draw or loss.  \nMaximize outcome: move to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N7, N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN6 -> N11, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N12, N6\nN0 -> N10, N11\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN13 -> N1, N9\nN1 -> N0, N5\nN8 -> N13, N4\nN12 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N7, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N2. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7 or N2. N7 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N7 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N7 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N12, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN11 -> N5, N9\nN13 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN12 -> N13, N4\nN1 -> N10, N2\nN6 -> N11, N7\nN10 -> N8, N3\n\nLegal moves: N10, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N10 or N2. We analyze each choice.  \nTo N10: from N10 → N8 (terminal) or N3 (terminal) ⇒ both lose the next player (no move left). So the player moving to N10 wins immediately after that.  \nTo N2: from N2 → N12 or N6.  \n- N12 → N13 (to N8 or N0 terminal) or N4 → then N12 or N12 → N13 or N12 → N4 → terminal chain.\n- N6 → N11 → N5 or N9 → both terminal or N5 → N4 or N0 (all terminal chains)  \nEventually all paths lead to terminal nodes.  \nBut critical insight: both paths from N2 eventually end in terminal nodes — so any chain from N2 is losing if you’re not careful.  \nBut if you play to N10, the opponent has no move from N10’s only destinations — thus they lose.  \nThus choosing N10 immediately forces a win. So choosing N10 leads to a win.  \nTherefore, to maximize outcome, move to N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6707130730050934, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N10 or N2. We analyze each choice.  \nTo N10: from N10 → N8 (terminal) or N3 (terminal) ⇒ both lose the next player (no move left). So the player moving to N10 wins immediately after that.  \nTo N2: from N2 → N12 or N6.  \n- N12 → N13 (to N8 or N0 terminal) or N4 → then N12 or N12 → N13 or N12 → N4 → terminal chain.\n- N6 → N11 → N5 or N9 → both terminal or N5 → N4 or N0 (all terminal chains)  \nEventually all paths lead to terminal nodes.  \nBut critical insight: both paths from N2 eventually end in terminal nodes — so any chain from N2 is losing if you’re not careful.  \nBut if you play to N10, the opponent has no move from N10’s only destinations — thus they lose.  \nThus choosing N10 immediately forces a win. So choosing N10 leads to a win.  \nTherefore, to maximize outcome, move to N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N12, N6\nN4 -> terminal\nN11 -> N5, N9\nN13 -> N8, N0\nN9 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N4\nN12 -> N13, N4\nN1 -> N10, N2\nN6 -> N11, N7\nN10 -> N8, N3\n\nLegal moves: N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N8 and N3. Both N8 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, either move ends the game on the next turn with a win. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N8 or N3 is equivalent. We select one.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.47699490662139216, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N8 and N3. Both N8 and N3 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, either move ends the game on the next turn with a win. Since both options result in an immediate win, either is optimal. Choosing N8 or N3 is equivalent. We select one.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN10 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N13\nN2 -> N9, N8\nN7 -> N4\nN1 -> N5, N11\nN11 -> N12, N6\nN8 -> N7, N10\nN0 -> N6\nN5 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N5, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N11.  \nExplore N1 → N5 → N3 → N7 → N4 → N13 (terminal): this is a path that leads to a win (opponent can't move after).  \nAlternatively, N1 → N5 → N3 → N2 → N9 (terminal): also terminal for opponent.  \nBut better, analyze N1 → N11 → N12 or N6 (both terminal), both end the game immediately. So both choices lead to terminal nodes, meaning the opponent has no move and we win.  \nThus, both N11 and N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes after a few steps.  \nBut if we choose N1 → N11, it leads directly to a terminal node (either N12 or N6) after one move, and opponent loses immediately.  \nN → N11 is shorter to terminal. So, choosing N1 → N11 ends the game immediately with win.  \nTherefore, choosing N1 → N11 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6426994906621393, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N5 or N11.  \nExplore N1 → N5 → N3 → N7 → N4 → N13 (terminal): this is a path that leads to a win (opponent can't move after).  \nAlternatively, N1 → N5 → N3 → N2 → N9 (terminal): also terminal for opponent.  \nBut better, analyze N1 → N11 → N12 or N6 (both terminal), both end the game immediately. So both choices lead to terminal nodes, meaning the opponent has no move and we win.  \nThus, both N11 and N5 eventually lead to terminal nodes after a few steps.  \nBut if we choose N1 → N11, it leads directly to a terminal node (either N12 or N6) after one move, and opponent loses immediately.  \nN → N11 is shorter to terminal. So, choosing N1 → N11 ends the game immediately with win.  \nTherefore, choosing N1 → N11 maximizes outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> terminal\nN3 -> N7, N2\nN9 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN10 -> N9, N0\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N13\nN2 -> N9, N8\nN7 -> N4\nN1 -> N5, N11\nN11 -> N12, N6\nN8 -> N7, N10\nN0 -> N6\nN5 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N12, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N12 and N6. N12 and N6 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both are winning moves. Either choice ends the game on the next turn. Since both result in an immediate win, either is optimal. However, choosing N6 or N12 is equivalent in outcome. N6 is directly terminal, so it's a stronger, simpler choice. Therefore, N6 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4557724957555178, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N12 and N6. N12 and N6 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both are winning moves. Either choice ends the game on the next turn. Since both result in an immediate win, either is optimal. However, choosing N6 or N12 is equivalent in outcome. N6 is directly terminal, so it's a stronger, simpler choice. Therefore, N6 is selected.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N12\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N3, N10, N7\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN13 -> N0, N11\nN4 -> N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN2 -> N13, N6\nN5 -> N3, N1\nN10 -> N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN12 -> N2, N4, N1\n\nLegal moves: N2, N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N2, N4, or N1. We analyze each destination for the possibility of forcing a win.  \nN1: leads to N→N3 or N→N7 (both end to terminal via N3→terminal or N7→terminal), so the next player loses — a win for current player.  \nN4 → N→N8 → terminal: so current player wins immediately.  \nN2 → N→N6 → N→N5 or N→N0 (N5→N3→terminal, N0→terminal); both lead to terminal.  \nThus, from N2, opponent has only terminal moves.  \nBut N12 to N1 is more direct and leads to immediate terminal, so opponent has no move, so win.  \nN→N4 → N→N8 → terminal, same win.  \nN→N2 leads to N→N→N6, which branches to N0 or N5, both terminal.  \nAll paths from N12 lead to terminal on next move unless a long loop is created.  \nBut the only way to force a win is to end the chain on your opponent’s turn.  \nFrom N12 to N1 or N4: both cause terminal in one step.  \nN→N2: then player can force opponent to lose in next.  \nBut N1 → N7 or N3 are both terminal paths — so if you go to N1, you force opponent into a terminal after their next move.  \nBest: N4 → terminal (direct win).  \nN→N4 → N→N8 → terminal: immediate loss for opponent after your move.  \nSo N4 is best, leads to immediate terminal, opponent has no move after your move.  \nHence, move to N4 wins.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.44094510469722703, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N2, N4, or N1. We analyze each destination for the possibility of forcing a win.  \nN1: leads to N→N3 or N→N7 (both end to terminal via N3→terminal or N7→terminal), so the next player loses — a win for current player.  \nN4 → N→N8 → terminal: so current player wins immediately.  \nN2 → N→N6 → N→N5 or N→N0 (N5→N3→terminal, N0→terminal); both lead to terminal.  \nThus, from N2, opponent has only terminal moves.  \nBut N12 to N1 is more direct and leads to immediate terminal, so opponent has no move, so win.  \nN→N4 → N→N8 → terminal, same win.  \nN→N2 leads to N→N→N6, which branches to N0 or N5, both terminal.  \nAll paths from N12 lead to terminal on next move unless a long loop is created.  \nBut the only way to force a win is to end the chain on your opponent’s turn.  \nFrom N12 to N1 or N4: both cause terminal in one step.  \nN→N2: then player can force opponent to lose in next.  \nBut N1 → N7 or N3 are both terminal paths — so if you go to N1, you force opponent into a terminal after their next move.  \nBest: N4 → terminal (direct win).  \nN→N4 → N→N8 → terminal: immediate loss for opponent after your move.  \nSo N4 is best, leads to immediate terminal, opponent has no move after your move.  \nHence, move to N4 wins.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game 