[2026-08-12 14:20:40] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N8, N3\nN0 -> N2, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each:</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": -0.4669779286926995, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N8 or N3. We analyze each:</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N8, N3\nN0 -> N2, N7\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN2 -> terminal\nN9 -> N6, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N9, N4\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N0\n\nLegal moves: N9, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N9 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N9 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N5\nN11 -> N2\nN2 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N7, N11, N4\nN13 -> N11, N1, N6\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N10, N4, N7\nN1 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N11, N1, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N11, N1, or N6.  \n- Move to N11: Then the path goes N13 → N11 → N2 → N3 → terminal. Player to move after N3 wins. So opponent wins if you reach N13 → N11.  \n- Move to N1: N13 → N1 → N8 → terminal; you win immediately. So move to N1 gives an immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N1 gives immediate win, and is optimal. </reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.2193831352574987, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N11, N1, or N6.  \n- Move to N11: Then the path goes N13 → N11 → N2 → N3 → terminal. Player to move after N3 wins. So opponent wins if you reach N13 → N11.  \n- Move to N1: N13 → N1 → N8 → terminal; you win immediately. So move to N1 gives an immediate win.  \nThus, moving to N1 gives immediate win, and is optimal. </reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N5\nN11 -> N2\nN2 -> N3\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N7, N11, N4\nN13 -> N11, N1, N6\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN3 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N10, N4, N7\nN1 -> N8\n\nLegal moves: N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the only legal move is to N8. N8 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N8 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N8 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N12\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN7 -> N3\nN11 -> N13, N8\nN13 -> N8\nN12 -> N10, N11\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N5\nN10 -> N7\nN5 -> N9\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N6. We analyze each choice with the goal of forcing a win (i.e., leaving the opponent in a losing position).  \nN4 → leads to N0→N4→N1 → then opponent moves from N1 to either N3 or N12.  \nFrom N3: terminal → opponent loses immediately.  \nFrom N12: N12 → N10 or N11  \nN10 → N7 → N3 (terminal) or N12 → N10 or N11 again.  \nEventually, both paths lead to cycles or terminal nodes with no backtracking.  \nBacktrack to N6: N0→N6 → N2 or N5  \nN6→N2 (terminal): opponent loses immediately  \n→ thus N→N2 wins directly  \nThus N6 leads to immediate win via N6→N2. So, choosing N6 leads to immediate win.  \nN4 leads to deeper branching with potential cycles or longer game.  \nThus, choosing N6 is optimal. Final outcome: win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6469439728353141, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N4 or N6. We analyze each choice with the goal of forcing a win (i.e., leaving the opponent in a losing position).  \nN4 → leads to N0→N4→N1 → then opponent moves from N1 to either N3 or N12.  \nFrom N3: terminal → opponent loses immediately.  \nFrom N12: N12 → N10 or N11  \nN10 → N7 → N3 (terminal) or N12 → N10 or N11 again.  \nEventually, both paths lead to cycles or terminal nodes with no backtracking.  \nBacktrack to N6: N0→N6 → N2 or N5  \nN6→N2 (terminal): opponent loses immediately  \n→ thus N→N2 wins directly  \nThus N6 leads to immediate win via N6→N2. So, choosing N6 leads to immediate win.  \nN4 leads to deeper branching with potential cycles or longer game.  \nThus, choosing N6 is optimal. Final outcome: win.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3, N12\nN4 -> N1, N12\nN7 -> N3\nN11 -> N13, N8\nN13 -> N8\nN12 -> N10, N11\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> N2, N5\nN10 -> N7\nN5 -> N9\nN2 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N2, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N2 and N5. N2 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N2 and N5. N2 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N11, N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN12 -> N4, N6\nN10 -> N3, N7\nN8 -> N0, N13\nN7 -> N11, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N1\nN9 -> N10, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8, N12\n\nLegal moves: N10, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N10 and N5. Analyze the outcome from each:  \nFrom N10 → moves to N3 or N7.  \nN3 → terminal (win), N7 → N11 or N0 both terminal. All paths from N10 reach terminal. So N10 leads to a win.  \nFrom N5 → moves to N8 or N12.  \nN12 → N4 or N6 (both terminal). N4 → N2 or N1 (both terminal). All paths from N5 lead to terminal. So N5 also ends in a win for the player who moves.  \nHowever, both paths result in a win for the current player. But since the game is impartial, the key is whether the opponent is forced to move into terminal first. All transitions end with no legal move for the other player. Both N10 and N5 are safe wins. But in a DAG with no cycles, a choice that ends the game in a forced win is optimal. Both paths are safe. But choosing one path that leads to a terminal faster? Both are equivalent in value. However, since N10 leads to more direct path with N0 or N3 (terminal early), but both are equivalent. So either move wins. Pick first one.  \nThus, pick N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6910865874363328, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N10 and N5. Analyze the outcome from each:  \nFrom N10 → moves to N3 or N7.  \nN3 → terminal (win), N7 → N11 or N0 both terminal. All paths from N10 reach terminal. So N10 leads to a win.  \nFrom N5 → moves to N8 or N12.  \nN12 → N4 or N6 (both terminal). N4 → N2 or N1 (both terminal). All paths from N5 lead to terminal. So N5 also ends in a win for the player who moves.  \nHowever, both paths result in a win for the current player. But since the game is impartial, the key is whether the opponent is forced to move into terminal first. All transitions end with no legal move for the other player. Both N10 and N5 are safe wins. But in a DAG with no cycles, a choice that ends the game in a forced win is optimal. Both paths are safe. But choosing one path that leads to a terminal faster? Both are equivalent in value. However, since N10 leads to more direct path with N0 or N3 (terminal early), but both are equivalent. So either move wins. Pick first one.  \nThus, pick N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> N11, N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN12 -> N4, N6\nN10 -> N3, N7\nN8 -> N0, N13\nN7 -> N11, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2, N1\nN9 -> N10, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8, N12\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4982173174872666, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N3 and N7. N3 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N3 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N3 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9\nN6 -> N5, N1\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N5\n\nLegal moves: N7, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N6.  \n- Path to N7: N7 → N4 → terminal → win (current player wins).\n- Path to N6: N6 → (N6→N5 or N6→N1)\n  - N6 → N5 → N9 → terminal (current player wins)\n  - N6 → N1 → N1→N3, N1→N0, N1→N0 → either way, N1→N0 → terminal (current player wins)\n\nAll paths lead to a terminal node via immediate or two-step sequences. The player who starts at N7 or N6 can win. But both choices allow the opponent to eventually face a terminal state after a forced chain.\n\nHowever, both paths eventually terminate in terminal states under perfect play. Therefore, each player has no choice to avoid losing. Thus, from the current node, no move leads to an immediate win or guarantee. Both paths are equivalent in terms of outcome (first player wins), so either is a draw in structure.\n\nSince we must choose a legal destination maximizing outcome, and no immediate advantage is available due to symmetry, selecting one such move leads to same result.\n\nN6 gives longer options but same result.\n\nHowever, N7 directly goes to terminal path in one step — this is better. N → N → terminal wins immediately, so N7 offers a direct path to immediate win. So, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7097623089983023, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N7 or N6.  \n- Path to N7: N7 → N4 → terminal → win (current player wins).\n- Path to N6: N6 → (N6→N5 or N6→N1)\n  - N6 → N5 → N9 → terminal (current player wins)\n  - N6 → N1 → N1→N3, N1→N0, N1→N0 → either way, N1→N0 → terminal (current player wins)\n\nAll paths lead to a terminal node via immediate or two-step sequences. The player who starts at N7 or N6 can win. But both choices allow the opponent to eventually face a terminal state after a forced chain.\n\nHowever, both paths eventually terminate in terminal states under perfect play. Therefore, each player has no choice to avoid losing. Thus, from the current node, no move leads to an immediate win or guarantee. Both paths are equivalent in terms of outcome (first player wins), so either is a draw in structure.\n\nSince we must choose a legal destination maximizing outcome, and no immediate advantage is available due to symmetry, selecting one such move leads to same result.\n\nN6 gives longer options but same result.\n\nHowever, N7 directly goes to terminal path in one step — this is better. N → N → terminal wins immediately, so N7 offers a direct path to immediate win. So, the optimal choice is N7.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> N9\nN6 -> N5, N1\nN1 -> N3, N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4, N0\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N5\n\nLegal moves: N4, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 and N0. N4 and N0 both lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both N4 and N0 are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize the outcome (win), either is optimal. Choosing N0 is valid and terminal-fast; however, both end the game immediately. Either choice wins. But as per maximizing outcome, either is equivalent. We select one.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.46001697792869267, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N4 and N0. N4 and N0 both lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both N4 and N0 are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize the outcome (win), either is optimal. Choosing N0 is valid and terminal-fast; however, both end the game immediately. Either choice wins. But as per maximizing outcome, either is equivalent. We select one.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}]
