nly move to direct to terminal. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.2923882286361065, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N10, N6, or N7. We analyze the outcome of each move in terms of the opponent's game from the destination nodes:  \n- N10 leads to N10: next player can go N2 or N7. N6 → N13 or N8, both lead to terminal. N2 → N11 (terminal) or N3. N3 leads to terminal. So, N6 or N10 is not safe.  \n- N7 leads to N4, terminal so opponent wins immediately.  \n- N10: leads to a complex path with many paths to terminal, but N9→N7 leads to immediate terminal.  \nSo, N7 ends the game immediately with the opponent's turn to move, meaning we have a win. N10 and N6 lead to further paths with potential draw or loss. N7 is the only move to direct to terminal. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> N6, N2, N7, N1\nN7 -> N4\nN13 -> N0, N2\nN11 -> terminal\nN6 -> N13, N8\nN1 -> N5\nN9 -> N10, N6, N7\nN3 -> N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN2 -> N11, N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N4. N4 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From