, N3 goes back to N13 or N7. From N10 or N3, N11 is eventually leads to terminal. N3 leads to N13 which leads to terminal. The goal is to force the opponent into a terminal from a dead end. However, N7 leads to N5 → N0 (to N9) → terminal, or N5 → N2 → N6 → N10 → N11 → terminal. The key is to see that N3 leads to N7 as a loop (N3 → N7 → N5 → N2 → N3 → N7 → N5...). So N7 leads to a looping and N3 also goes to loop, but N7 leads to N5 which eventually forces to N2 and N6. From this, N3 is a way to go into loop and N7 leads to N5 and back. Since both lead to loops, the player who can control to lead to the opposite end to force a losing position wins. But from N4, moving to N7, you lead to N5 → N2 → N11 which ends terminal. But in reverse, if the opponent moves to N3 → N13 → N8 or N1 which ends immediately. The optimal is to move to N3. From N4, going to N3, the opponent must go through N7, and if N7 goes to N5 and then N2, N3 again to N7, N2 to N11, then N terminal. So to maximize, move to N3. </isolate>  \n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N7 and N3. Following the graph, N7 leads to N5, and then N5 has a path to N0 and N2. N2 continues to N6, N3 goes back to N13 or N7. From N10 or N3, N11 is eventually leads to terminal. N3 leads to N13 which leads to terminal. The goal is to force the opponent into a terminal from a dead end. However, N7 leads to N5 → N0 (to N9) → terminal, or N5 → N2 → N6 → N10 → N11 → terminal. The key is to see that N3 leads to N7 as a loop (N3 → N7 → N5 → N2 → N3 → N7 → N5...). So N7 leads to a looping and N3 also goes to loop, but N7 leads to N5 which eventually forces to N2 and N6. From this, N3 is a way to go into loop and N7 leads to N5 and back. Since both lead to loops, the player who can control to lead to the opposite end to force a losing position wins. But from N4, moving to N7, you lead to N5 → N2 → N11 which ends terminal. But in reverse, if the opponent moves to N3 →