[2026-08-12 14:26:58] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N5\nN1 -> N3, N9\nN2 -> N6, N8\nN6 -> N7\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N8\n\nLegal moves: N3, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N3 and N9. We need to analyze the game outcome from each move to determine which leads to a win. Since the graph is directed and acyclic and terminal nodes are losing positions, we analyze backwards using backward induction.  \n- If you move to a terminal, the opponent has no move (you win).  \n- From N9 → N4 (terminal) or N8 (terminal). So both options lead to opponent winning on next move. So N9 leads to terminal from next node, so the player who arrives at N9 moves to N4 or N8 (terminal), meaning the other player loses. Therefore, the player who moves to N9 wins.  \n- From N3 → N0 → N2, N5. N2 leads to N6 then N7 (terminal). N3 to N0 to N5 leads to terminal (N5→ terminal). So any move from N0 leads to opponent winning from the other paths.  \nSo from N1, moving to N9 leads to a win (since both branches from N9 terminate). From N3, eventually the opponent can reach a terminal (N0 → N2 → N6 → N7, or N5 → terminal). In all cases, the game ends and the opponent wins if you move to N3. Therefore, choosing N3 leads to a losing outcome. Choosing N9 leads to a winning move. So optimal is to go to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7097623089983023, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N3 and N9. We need to analyze the game outcome from each move to determine which leads to a win. Since the graph is directed and acyclic and terminal nodes are losing positions, we analyze backwards using backward induction.  \n- If you move to a terminal, the opponent has no move (you win).  \n- From N9 → N4 (terminal) or N8 (terminal). So both options lead to opponent winning on next move. So N9 leads to terminal from next node, so the player who arrives at N9 moves to N4 or N8 (terminal), meaning the other player loses. Therefore, the player who moves to N9 wins.  \n- From N3 → N0 → N2, N5. N2 leads to N6 then N7 (terminal). N3 to N0 to N5 leads to terminal (N5→ terminal). So any move from N0 leads to opponent winning from the other paths.  \nSo from N1, moving to N9 leads to a win (since both branches from N9 terminate). From N3, eventually the opponent can reach a terminal (N0 → N2 → N6 → N7, or N5 → terminal). In all cases, the game ends and the opponent wins if you move to N3. Therefore, choosing N3 leads to a losing outcome. Choosing N9 leads to a winning move. So optimal is to go to N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2, N5\nN1 -> N3, N9\nN2 -> N6, N8\nN6 -> N7\nN3 -> N0, N9\nN4 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN9 -> N4, N8\n\nLegal moves: N4, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N4 and N8. N4 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4999151103565365, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N4 and N8. N4 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N12, N13\nN0 -> N1, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN13 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N7\nN6 -> N3, N13\nN7 -> N0, N5\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN3 -> N2, N11\nN11 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- From N4: the path continues to N9 or N8, both leading to terminal (win for the player who moves there).  \n- From N4 → N9 → terminal or N4 → N8 → terminal. Thus, both terminal nodes are \"safe\" (the opponent loses immediately).  \n- From N7 → N0 (then to N1 or N6), or N7 → N5 (then to N12 or N13, both terminal).  \n  So, from N7, either way eventually leads to a terminal node.  \nBut N10 → N5 via N7 → N5 → N12 or N13: both end in terminal.  \nSo either way, from N7, opponent is forced to go to terminal.  \nBut key is to force opponent into terminal.  \n\nFrom N4:  \n- N4 → N12? no.  \n- N4 → N9 or N8: both go to terminal.  \n→ Opponent has no move.  \n→ Win!  \n\nSo from N4 you win immediately by making opponent face terminal.\n\nThus, moving to N4 is better than N7, which leads to terminal only after a longer path, but N4 ends game immediately.  \nThus, N4 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6927843803056026, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- From N4: the path continues to N9 or N8, both leading to terminal (win for the player who moves there).  \n- From N4 → N9 → terminal or N4 → N8 → terminal. Thus, both terminal nodes are \"safe\" (the opponent loses immediately).  \n- From N7 → N0 (then to N1 or N6), or N7 → N5 (then to N12 or N13, both terminal).  \n  So, from N7, either way eventually leads to a terminal node.  \nBut N10 → N5 via N7 → N5 → N12 or N13: both end in terminal.  \nSo either way, from N7, opponent is forced to go to terminal.  \nBut key is to force opponent into terminal.  \n\nFrom N4:  \n- N4 → N12? no.  \n- N4 → N9 or N8: both go to terminal.  \n→ Opponent has no move.  \n→ Win!  \n\nSo from N4 you win immediately by making opponent face terminal.\n\nThus, moving to N4 is better than N7, which leads to terminal only after a longer path, but N4 ends game immediately.  \nThus, N4 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N12, N13\nN0 -> N1, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N9, N8\nN13 -> terminal\nN10 -> N4, N7\nN6 -> N3, N13\nN7 -> N0, N5\nN12 -> terminal\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN3 -> N2, N11\nN11 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N9 and N8 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn with a win. Since either move wins, any is optimal. Choosing N9 is sufficient.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4880305602716469, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9 and N8. N9 and N8 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn with a win. Since either move wins, any is optimal. Choosing N9 is sufficient.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N3, N6\nN6 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N2\nN5 -> N1\nN8 -> N7, N0\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N3 and N6.  \n- If the token moves to N3, the opponent can go to N8 or N0.  \n    - If to N8, then opponent can move to N7 (win) or N0 (which goes to N9 or N2). N2 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins from N2.  \n    - If to N0, opponent can go to N2 → terminal or N0 → loop. But N0 leads to terminal or N2, which are both terminal.  \n    Thus, N3 leads to terminal paths eventually for the opponent to win.  \n- If move to N6, path:  \n    - N6 → N5 (N5 → N1) leads to terminal or N6 → N9 (terminal)  \n    So all paths from N6 lead to terminal.  \n\nTherefore, both move paths lead to terminal eventually. But from N4, if the opponent is at terminal, they lose. So if you force them into terminal via N3 → N8/N0 → terminal, or N6 → N5/N9 → terminal, they are already losing. But we need to maximize control. The critical insight is the path via N6 ends with immediate terminal moves — both N9 and N5 end at terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 wins by forcing the opponent to lose on the next step. Thus N6 leads to immediate terminal for the opponent.  \nSo N6 is better than N3 because it leads to immediate terminal.  \nFinal decision: Move to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7335314091680816, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N3 and N6.  \n- If the token moves to N3, the opponent can go to N8 or N0.  \n    - If to N8, then opponent can move to N7 (win) or N0 (which goes to N9 or N2). N2 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins from N2.  \n    - If to N0, opponent can go to N2 → terminal or N0 → loop. But N0 leads to terminal or N2, which are both terminal.  \n    Thus, N3 leads to terminal paths eventually for the opponent to win.  \n- If move to N6, path:  \n    - N6 → N5 (N5 → N1) leads to terminal or N6 → N9 (terminal)  \n    So all paths from N6 lead to terminal.  \n\nTherefore, both move paths lead to terminal eventually. But from N4, if the opponent is at terminal, they lose. So if you force them into terminal via N3 → N8/N0 → terminal, or N6 → N5/N9 → terminal, they are already losing. But we need to maximize control. The critical insight is the path via N6 ends with immediate terminal moves — both N9 and N5 end at terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N6 wins by forcing the opponent to lose on the next step. Thus N6 leads to immediate terminal for the opponent.  \nSo N6 is better than N3 because it leads to immediate terminal.  \nFinal decision: Move to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN3 -> N8, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N3, N6\nN6 -> N5, N9\nN9 -> terminal\nN0 -> N9, N2\nN5 -> N1\nN8 -> N7, N0\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N5 and N9. N9 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N5 and N9. N9 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N9 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N9.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN3 -> N8, N1, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N8, N7\nN5 -> N9\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N1, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N5. We analyze each destination:  \n- N3 → N8: N8 leads to N4 (terminal) or N2. N2 → N0 → terminal (loss if we can't escape). So if opponent plays optimally, we will lose from N2.  \n- N3 → N1: N1 leads to N4 or N8 or N7. N4 or N7 are terminal, N8 leads to N2 or N4, which is a loss if you can't escape.  \n- N3 → N5: N5 → N9 → N6 → terminal. This is a win.  \n\nHence, the move to N5 is optimal because it leads directly to terminal with no escape. So N5 leads to an immediate win for the player who moves to it. This maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.7211941143180531, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N8, N1, or N5. We analyze each destination:  \n- N3 → N8: N8 leads to N4 (terminal) or N2. N2 → N0 → terminal (loss if we can't escape). So if opponent plays optimally, we will lose from N2.  \n- N3 → N1: N1 leads to N4 or N8 or N7. N4 or N7 are terminal, N8 leads to N2 or N4, which is a loss if you can't escape.  \n- N3 → N5: N5 → N9 → N6 → terminal. This is a win.  \n\nHence, the move to N5 is optimal because it leads directly to terminal with no escape. So N5 leads to an immediate win for the player who moves to it. This maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN3 -> N8, N1, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N8, N7\nN5 -> N9\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the only legal move is to N9. From N9, the next move is to N6, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N9 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N9 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.48378607809847196, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the only legal move is to N9. From N9, the next move is to N6, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N9 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N9 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N6\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN3 -> N8, N1, N5\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N8, N7\nN5 -> N9\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N6. N6 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N11\nN3 -> N1, N8\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N17\nN1 -> terminal\nN16 -> N4, N6\nN17 -> N16, N14\nN15 -> terminal\nN14 -> terminal\nN2 -> N9, N5\nN0 -> N3, N15\nN8 -> terminal\nN13 -> N2, N16\nN12 -> N9, N3\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> N13\nN5 -> N0, N12\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N10, N17assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N10 and N17.  \n- N10 goes to N13, then to either N2 or N16.  \n    - N2 → N9 → N11 → terminal, or N9 → N11 → terminal.  \n    - N16 → N4 → terminal or N16 → N6 → terminal.  \n- N17 → N16, then N4 or N to N16 → N1 → terminal or N16 → N15 → terminal.  \nBoth paths lead to terminal states.  \nThe path via N17 allows a win for the player forcing the move to N17.  \nTherefore, N17 leads to terminal win in a forced game.  \nThus, selecting N17 maximizes the game outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N17</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6011035653650254, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N10 and N17.  \n- N10 goes to N13, then to either N2 or N16.  \n    - N2 → N9 → N11 → terminal, or N9 → N11 → terminal.  \n    - N16 → N4 → terminal or N16 → N6 → terminal.  \n- N17 → N16, then N4 or N to N16 → N1 → terminal or N16 → N15 → terminal.  \nBoth paths lead to terminal states.  \nThe path via N17 allows a win for the player forcing the move to N17.  \nTherefore, N17 leads to terminal win in a forced game.  \nThus, selecting N17 maximizes the game outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N17</answer>"}]
