[2026-08-12 14:28:17] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N5\nN5 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6\nN1 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> N0, N4\n\nLegal moves: N0, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N0 and N4.  \n- Path to N0: N0→N1→N9→N3 or N5→N7. From N0: (N0→N1→N9→N5→N7 or N3). N7 leads to terminal, N3 leads to terminal. The player who moves to either N5 or N9 eventually can be trapped. But N2 → N0 leads to N0's path which leads to N1 which can move to N9, or N7, both ending in terminal.  \n- Path to N4: N4 → N6 → terminal. So one player can win.  \nN0 offers longer path. N2→N0→N4→N6→terminal.  \nBut, by going to N0, the opponent can move to N1→N9→N3 or N5→N7. If you go to N0, you force opponent to choose paths which will eventually end with no moves.  \nBut by going to N4, the path ends immediately after: N4 → N6 → terminal. So the opponent has no move from N4’s extension. Therefore, N4 results in an immediate end. So the player who moves to N4 wins (opponent moves after N2 to N4, then the opponent loses).  \nN2 → N4 leads to terminal after a single move, so the opponent loses immediately. Therefore, this is a winning move. N0 is a trap, leads to longer game. N4 ends faster and gives the player a win immediately after.  \nTherefore, N4 maximizes game outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7454159592529712, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N0 and N4.  \n- Path to N0: N0→N1→N9→N3 or N5→N7. From N0: (N0→N1→N9→N5→N7 or N3). N7 leads to terminal, N3 leads to terminal. The player who moves to either N5 or N9 eventually can be trapped. But N2 → N0 leads to N0's path which leads to N1 which can move to N9, or N7, both ending in terminal.  \n- Path to N4: N4 → N6 → terminal. So one player can win.  \nN0 offers longer path. N2→N0→N4→N6→terminal.  \nBut, by going to N0, the opponent can move to N1→N9→N3 or N5→N7. If you go to N0, you force opponent to choose paths which will eventually end with no moves.  \nBut by going to N4, the path ends immediately after: N4 → N6 → terminal. So the opponent has no move from N4’s extension. Therefore, N4 results in an immediate end. So the player who moves to N4 wins (opponent moves after N2 to N4, then the opponent loses).  \nN2 → N4 leads to terminal after a single move, so the opponent loses immediately. Therefore, this is a winning move. N0 is a trap, leads to longer game. N4 ends faster and gives the player a win immediately after.  \nTherefore, N4 maximizes game outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N5\nN5 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6\nN1 -> N9, N7\nN2 -> N0, N4\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the only legal move is to N6. From N6, the next player must move to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately because the opponent 