rminal) or N9 (terminal), so the opponent will lose. Thus, the player forces a win.  \n- From N10, the opponent can move to N1 (terminal) or N0 which leads back to N1, which again leads to terminal. This means the player is also forced to lose.  \n\nSo moving to N5 is optimal.  A player who moves to N5 has a winning strategy. </reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> N7, N0\nN4 -> terminal\nN10 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N1, N12\nN1 -> N2, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN8 -> N11, N7\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N10, N5\nN7 -> N13, N4\nN9 -> terminal\nN11 -> N6\nN13 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1\n\nLegal moves: N1, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1 and N12.  \nN1 leads to N1, N2 or N9. N1 leads to N2, terminal (lose), or N9 (lose). N2 leads to terminal. So N1 is a losing move. N9 and N2 are terminal moves, meaning the player who moves to these ends the game and wins if they can force the opponent into a no-move.  \nBut if you move to N1 → you lose if the opponent plays optimally (they can always respond to a move toward terminal).  \nN12 goes to terminal. So you immediately move the opponent to terminal: the player to move after you loses. So by going to N12 you win immediately.  \nSo playing to N12 leads to terminal on the opponent’s turn → opponent loses → we win.  \nThus, move to N12 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 1.387011884550085, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N1 and N12.  \nN1 leads to N1, N2 or N9. N1 leads 