., force a win), either move is optimal. However, N6 leads directly to terminal, ending the game immediately. Therefore, moving to N6 is a stronger choice as it ends the game with a win without delay. Hence, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.449830220713073, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, the legal moves are to N10 and N6. N10 and N6 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both are winning moves. Since the goal is to maximize the outcome (i.e., force a win), either move is optimal. However, N6 leads directly to terminal, ending the game immediately. Therefore, moving to N6 is a stronger choice as it ends the game with a win without delay. Hence, N6 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N12, N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2\nN7 -> N2, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN10 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N7, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N10, N0\nN3 -> N5, N11\n\nLegal moves: N9, N7, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N9, N7, or N4.  \n- From N9 → N3. From N3: to N5 (terminal) or N11. From N11: to N12 → N0, or to N1 → terminal or N8 → terminal. Both N1, N8, N0 are terminal. N5 → terminal. So paths from N3 are losing.  \n- From N7 → N2 → terminal. Also N2 → terminal. So N7 leads to a win (no further moves for the opponent).  \n- From N4 → N2 (terminal). Again, terminal.  \n\nBut, in vertex geography, you want to **force the opponent into a terminal position**. N7 leads to terminal move (N7 → N2 → ends), so it's a direct loss for the next player (i.e. you win immediately by forcing opponent). N4 → N2 → terminal also ends opponent's turn.  \nBut the critical path is: N13 → N7 → N2 → game ends (opponent has no move).  \n\nHowever, consider N9 → N3 → N5 or N11:  \n- N3 leads to N5 → terminal or N11 → N0, or N12 → N0 (or N1, N8 all terminal). All leads to terminal or terminal-like. All paths from N11, N9, etc., lead to terminal. So, all are safe.  \n\nBut **only a move to a node which leads to terminal via a forced move for opponent is good**. But N7 and N4 immediately lead to terminal.  \nBut from N13 → N11 is not available.  \nN11 leads to N12 → N0 (terminal), N0 ends game. So all paths from any such node eventually end. So if you go to N7:  \n- N13 → N7 → next player must go from N2 → already terminal. No move? So they lose right away after your move to N7 → game ends at N13→N7→no further move available from N2. So **N7 gives a win (no next move for the opponent).  \nThus, **N7 is the optimal move**: it leads to terminal immediately and gives opponent zero moves.  \n\nThus, the optimal choice is N7.</p>  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.5487549518958688, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the legal moves are to N9, N7, or N4.  \n- From N9 → N3. From N3: to N5 (terminal) or N11. From N11: to N12 → N0, or to N1 → terminal or N8 → terminal. Both N1, N8, N0 are terminal. N5 → terminal. So paths from N3 are losing.  \n- From N7 → N2 → terminal. Also N2 → terminal. So N7 leads to a win (no further moves for the opponent).  \n- From N4 → N2 (terminal). Again, terminal.  \n\nBut, in vertex geography, you want to **force the opponent into a terminal position**. N7 leads to terminal move (N7 → N2 → ends), so it's a direct loss for the next player (i.e. you win immediately by forcing opponent). N4 → N2 → terminal also ends opponent's turn.  \nBut the critical path is: N13 → N7 → N2 → game ends (opponent has no move).  \n\nHowever, consider N9 → N3 → N5 or N11:  \n- N3 leads to N5 → terminal or N11 → N0, or N12 → N0 (or N1, N8 all terminal). All leads to terminal or terminal-like. All paths from N11, N9, etc., lead to terminal. So, all are safe.  \n\nBut **only a move to a node which leads to terminal via a forced move for opponent is good**. But N7 and N4 immediately lead to terminal.  \nBut from N13 → N11 is not available.  \nN11 leads to N12 → N0 (terminal), N0 ends game. So all paths from any such node eventually end. So if you go to N7:  \n- N13 → N7 → next player must go from N2 → already terminal. No move? So they lose right away after your move to N7 → game ends at N13→N7→no further move available from N2. So **N7 gives a win (no next move for the opponent).  \nThus, **N7 is the optimal move**: it leads to terminal immediately and gives opponent zero moves.  \n\nThus, the optimal choice is N7.</p>  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN11 -> N12, N1, N8\nN0 -> terminal\nN12 -> N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N2\nN7 -> N2, N6\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3\nN10 -> terminal\nN13 -> N9, N7, N4\nN1 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N10, N0\nN3 -> N5, N11\n\nLegal moves: N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N2 and N6. N2 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N2 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N2 is a winning move. 