> N9, N5, N1\nN2 -> N6\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N4\nN8 -> N0, N5, N4\nN4 -> N2, N7\nN9 -> N3\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N5, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the player can move to N0, N5, or N4. We analyze the outcome of each move using backward induction to find which destination leads to a winning position for the player to move next (i.e., where the opponent is forced into a losing position).  \n- N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (game ends immediately). The opponent has no move, so we win.  \n- N5 → N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (opponent wins from N4). So this leads to a loss.  \n- N0 → N9 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins). Or N0 → N5 → N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (opponent wins). Either way, opponent wins.  \nOnly N4 leads to a forced win for the player. So choosing N4 ensures victory.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.27031692133559715, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the player can move to N0, N5, or N4. We analyze the outcome of each move using backward induction to find which destination leads to a winning position for the player to move next (i.e., where the opponent is forced into a losing position).  \n- N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (game ends immediately). The opponent has no move, so we win.  \n- N5 → N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (opponent wins from N4). So this leads to a loss.  \n- N0 → N9 → N3 → terminal (opponent wins). Or N0 → N5 → N4 → N2 → N6 → terminal (opponent wins). Either way, opponent wins.  \nOnly N4 leads to a forced win for the player. So choosing N4 ensures victory.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not foll