s immediately. However, from N2, the player may reach N9 → N3, which is terminal, or N9 → N1 → N6, which leads to terminal. So every path from N2 or N9 eventually leads to terminal. So from N5, choosing N2 or N7 both result in losing positions if the opponent plays perfectly. But to maximize outcome, the key is to force opponent to face terminal. N7 leads to terminal immediately — the opponent has no move — so the player who moves to N7 wins because the opponent must move to a terminal. Hence, moving to N7 is winning as the next player loses immediately. Thus, N7 is better than N2 (which leads to more branching, but also ends). Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7029711375212224, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N2 and N7. From N2, the player can move to N9 or N6. From N6, the player moves to N3 (which leads to terminal), or to N4 (terminal). From N3 or N4 or N8 or N0, the game ends immediately. From N9, the player goes to N3 (terminal) or N1. N1 leads to N6 (terminal or N3/N4) or N8 (terminal). N7 leads directly to N0 which is terminal. Thus, moving to N7 leads to an immediate terminal state and so the player loses immediately. However, from N2, the player may reach N9 → N3, which is terminal, or N9 → N1 → N6, which leads to terminal. So every path from N2 or N9 eventually leads to terminal. So from N5, choosing N2 or N7 both result in losing positions if the opponent plays perfectly. But to maximize outcome, the key is to force opponent to face terminal. N7 leads to terminal immediately — the opponent has no move — so the player who moves to N7 wins because the opponent must move to a terminal. Hence, moving to N7 is winning as the next player loses immediately. Thus, N7 is better than N2 (which leads to more branching, but also ends). Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> N3, N4\nN1 -> N6, N8\nN5 -> N2, N7\nN2 -> N9, N6\nN3 -> terminal\nN7 -> N0\nN9 -> N3, N1\n\nLegal moves: N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N0, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N0 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N0, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N0 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N0 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N17, N15\nN11 -> N13, N1\nN1 -> N9, N0\nN3 -> N8\nN10 -> terminal\nN15 -> N17, N2\nN13 -> N5, N7\nN7 -> N14\nN12 -> N5\nN0 -> N12, N10\nN17 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N16\nN8 -> terminal\nN14 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N14, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN16 -> N11\n\nLegal moves: N4, N16assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4 and N16.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to a position where the opponent controls the game, with the graph leading to terminal states through chains.  \n- Moving to N16: leads to N11, which has access to N13 (to N5 or N7).  \n  N7 → N14 (terminal) and N5 → terminal, but from N13 → N5 or N7, both terminal.  \n  Also, from N12 → N5, terminal.  \n  So from N16 → N11 → N13 → N5 or N7 → terminal (opponent wins).  \nHowever, the game ends when the player cannot move — the opponent must eventually lose.  \nFrom N4:  \nN4 → N17 or N15: N17 → terminal, N15 → N17 or N2 (both terminal).  \nFrom N4, all paths lead to terminal, so opponent has no move. Player who moves to N4 ends game.  \nN6 → N16 → N11 → N13 → N7 → N14 (terminal) — this path also ends.  \nBut crucially, if N6 → N4, immediate paths end.  \nThus, moving to N4 allows **immediate end to opponent’s turn**, meaning **you win by forcing a loss**.\n\nSo, choosing N4 **forces a win**, by ending the game on the next turn.\n\nHence, N4 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.7318336162988115, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N4 and N16.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to a position where the opponent controls the game, with the graph leading to terminal states through chains.  \n- Moving to N16: leads to N11, which has access to N13 (to N5 or N7).  \n  N7 → N14 (terminal) and N5 → terminal, but from N13 → N5 or N7, both terminal.  \n  Also, from N12 → N5, terminal.  \n  So from N16 → N11 → N13 → N5 or N7 → terminal (opponent wins).  \nHowever, the game ends when the player cannot move — the opponent must eventually lose.  \nFrom N4:  \nN4 → N17 or N15: N17 → terminal, N15 → N17 or N2 (both terminal).  \nFrom N4, all paths lead to terminal, so opponent has no move. Player who moves to N4 ends game.  \nN6 → N16 → N11 → N13 → N7 → N14 (terminal) — this path also ends.  \nBut crucially, if N6 → N4, immediate paths end.  \nThus, moving to N4 allows **immediate end to opponent’s turn**, meaning **you win by forcing a loss**.\n\nSo, choosing N4 **forces a win**, by ending the game on the next turn.\n\nHence, N4 is the optimal choice.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N17, N15\nN11 -> N13, N1\nN1 -> N9, N0\nN3 -> N8\nN10 -> terminal\nN15 -> N17, N2\nN13 -> N5, N7\nN7 -> N14\nN12 -> N5\nN0 -> N12, N10\nN17 -> terminal\nN6 -> N4, N16\nN8 -> terminal\nN14 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN9 -> N14, N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN16 -> N11\n\nLegal moves: N17, N15assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N17 and N15. N17 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N17 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N17 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N17.</reason>\n<answer>N17</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4931239388794566, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N17 and N15. N17 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N17 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N17 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N17.</reason>\n<answer>N17</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process con