1 -> N13, N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N1 or N2.  \nN2 leads to N5 (terminal), so the player who moves to N2 will win immediately (the opponent has no move).  \nN1 leads to N13 or N8.  \nN13 leads to N7 or N0.  \nN0 leads to N12 (terminal) or N6.  \nN6 leads to terminal.  \nN8 leads to N4 → N3 → N9 or N10 (both terminal).  \nHence, the path N1 → N8 → N4 → N3 → N9 ends with a terminal move.  \nBut N2 ends the game immediately on the next player's turn—so the player who takes N2 wins (the opponent has no move).  \nThus, moving to N2 immediately ends the game with the opponent losing.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 is a winning move.  \nFinal optimal move: N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6223259762308999, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N1 or N2.  \nN2 leads to N5 (terminal), so the player who moves to N2 will win immediately (the opponent has no move).  \nN1 leads to N13 or N8.  \nN13 leads to N7 or N0.  \nN0 leads to N12 (terminal) or N6.  \nN6 leads to terminal.  \nN8 leads to N4 → N3 → N9 or N10 (both terminal).  \nHence, the path N1 → N8 → N4 → N3 → N9 ends with a terminal move.  \nBut N2 ends the game immediately on the next player's turn—so the player who takes N2 wins (the opponent has no move).  \nThus, moving to N2 immediately ends the game with the opponent losing.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 is a winning move.  \nFinal optimal move: N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this