[2026-08-12 14:41:18] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN13 -> N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN10 -> N0, N8, N11\nN4 -> N12, N6, N13, N1\nN3 -> N0, N10\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N2, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N9, N7\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N13, N2, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N13, N2, or N3.  \n- N13 leads to terminal, so the player who moves there wins immediately (the opponent has no move).  \n- N3 leads to N0 (terminal) or N10, where N10 branches to N0 (terminal), N8 (terminal), or N11 (terminal). All terminal. So N3 leads to a losing position for the opponent if you can avoid cycles or immediate terminal.  \n- N2 leads to N4, N9 (terminal), or N7 (terminal). From N4 we can go to N12 (terminal), N6 (terminal), N13 (then terminal), or N1 (terminal). So N2 opens a rich path that can be exploited.  \nBut the best move is to go to N13: it immediately ends the opponent’s turn with a move to a terminal node, meaning the opponent has no further move, so the player who moves to N13 wins directly. Thus, N13 leads to immediate victory.  \nThus, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": 1.6804470854555744, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N13, N2, or N3.  \n- N13 leads to terminal, so the player who moves there wins immediately (the opponent has no move).  \n- N3 leads to N0 (terminal) or N10, where N10 branches to N0 (terminal), N8 (terminal), or N11 (terminal). All terminal. So N3 leads to a losing position for the opponent if you can avoid cycles or immediate terminal.  \n- N2 leads to N4, N9 (terminal), or N7 (terminal). From N4 we can go to N12 (terminal), N6 (terminal), N13 (then terminal), or N1 (terminal). So N2 opens a rich path that can be exploited.  \nBut the best move is to go to N13: it immediately ends the opponent’s turn with a move to a terminal node, meaning the opponent has no further move, so the player who moves to N13 wins directly. Thus, N13 leads to immediate victory.  \nThus, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN13 -> N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN10 -> N0, N8, N11\nN4 -> N12, N6, N13, N1\nN3 -> N0, N10\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N2, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N9, N7\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N13, the only legal move is to N3. From N3, the player can move to N0 or N10. N0 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Thus, moving to N10 is better. From N10, the options are to N0 (terminal), N8 (terminal), or N11 (terminal). All are terminal. Thus, moving to N10 leads to a forced win for the opponent if they play optimally. Therefore, from N13, the only move is to N3—this leads to a losing position for the player who moves. Thus, the best choice is to move to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 0.42860780984719865, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N13, the only legal move is to N3. From N3, the player can move to N0 or N10. N0 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins immediately. Thus, moving to N10 is better. From N10, the options are to N0 (terminal), N8 (terminal), or N11 (terminal). All are terminal. Thus, moving to N10 leads to a forced win for the opponent if they play optimally. Therefore, from N13, the only move is to N3—this leads to a losing position for the player who moves. Thus, the best choice is to move to N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN13 -> N3\nN12 -> terminal\nN10 -> N0, N8, N11\nN4 -> N12, N6, N13, N1\nN3 -> N0, N10\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N13, N2, N3\nN11 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N4, N9, N7\nN9 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N0, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N0 and N10. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N0 and N10. N0 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N11\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N2\nN8 -> N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N13\nN11 -> N8, N10, N3\nN12 -> N7, N9, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> N4, N0, N10, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N8, N10, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N8, N10, or N3.  \n- Moving to N10 leads to N8 (to N5 or terminal). N8 leads to N5, which is terminal. So N10 → N8 → N5 → terminal.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N5 (terminal).  \n- Moving to N3 leads to branching: N3 → N4, N0 (terminal), N10, or N12.  \n    - N3 → N4 → N7 → N13 or N1 → both terminal.  \n    - N3 → N10 → N8 → N5 (terminal).  \n    - N3 → N12 → N7 → N13, or N9 → N2 → terminal, or N6 → terminal. So N12 can lead to a terminal or a longer chain but all lead to terminals.  \nThus, N11 → N3 gives more branching and control but ultimately leads to terminal. The key is to extend the path and force the opponent into a losing position.  \nHowever, all paths lead to terminal nodes due to terminal states in each subtree.  \nSince players alternate and eventually someone must move into terminal, **the player who first moves into a dead-end (terminal) loses** if no alternate is available.  \nBecause each branch from N3 leads to a terminal after a few moves, the player who chooses N3 allows the opponent to go further and eventually reach a terminal.  \nInstead, the goal is to *force the opponent into a terminal move.*  \nCritical: N3 → N4 → N7 → N13 (terminal).  \nN12 → N7 → N13, or N9 → N2 (terminal).  \nNo cycles or safe loops exist. All chains lead to terminal.  \nSo, all paths end in losing.  \nBest move: **N3**, as it allows more options and delays the end, potentially setting a more difficult path for the opponent.  \nTherefore, to maximize advantage, choosing N3 leads to the most active game play.  \n</reason>\n\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.49357668364459545, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N11, the legal moves are to N8, N10, or N3.  \n- Moving to N10 leads to N8 (to N5 or terminal). N8 leads to N5, which is terminal. So N10 → N8 → N5 → terminal.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N5 (terminal).  \n- Moving to N3 leads to branching: N3 → N4, N0 (terminal), N10, or N12.  \n    - N3 → N4 → N7 → N13 or N1 → both terminal.  \n    - N3 → N10 → N8 → N5 (terminal).  \n    - N3 → N12 → N7 → N13, or N9 → N2 → terminal, or N6 → terminal. So N12 can lead to a terminal or a longer chain but all lead to terminals.  \nThus, N11 → N3 gives more branching and control but ultimately leads to terminal. The key is to extend the path and force the opponent into a losing position.  \nHowever, all paths lead to terminal nodes due to terminal states in each subtree.  \nSince players alternate and eventually someone must move into terminal, **the player who first moves into a dead-end (terminal) loses** if no alternate is available.  \nBecause each branch from N3 leads to a terminal after a few moves, the player who chooses N3 allows the opponent to go further and eventually reach a terminal.  \nInstead, the goal is to *force the opponent into a terminal move.*  \nCritical: N3 → N4 → N7 → N13 (terminal).  \nN12 → N7 → N13, or N9 → N2 (terminal).  \nNo cycles or safe loops exist. All chains lead to terminal.  \nSo, all paths end in losing.  \nBest move: **N3**, as it allows more options and delays the end, potentially setting a more difficult path for the opponent.  \nTherefore, to maximize advantage, choosing N3 leads to the most active game play.  \n</reason>\n\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N2\nN8 -> N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN7 -> N13\nN11 -> N8, N10, N3\nN12 -> N7, N9, N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7, N1\nN3 -> N4, N0, N10, N12\nN5 -> terminal\nN10 -> N8\nN2 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N0, N10, N12assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N4, N0, N10, or N12. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4914261460101867, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N3, the legal moves are to N4, N0, N10, or N12. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N8\nN7 -> N9, N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1\nN3 -> N2, N9, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN9 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N9, N3, or N5.  \n- N9 leads to N4, then N3 or N4 (N4 → N6 or N8) eventually leads to terminal.  \n- N3 → N2 (terminal), N3 → N9 → N4 → N6 (terminal) or N4 → N8 → N0 (terminal).  \n- N5 → N1 → terminal.  \n\nAll moves lead to terminal eventually. So, each path ends after some moves.  \nBut key: we aim to **maximize the game outcome** — the player who forces the opponent into the terminal node (no legal move) wins.  \nAll paths are directed and end in terminals.  \nThe length of paths varies. A longer path allows more moves. Since player who has no move loses, **extending the game (length) increases control** — if the game is longer, the first player can force the opponent to lose in a longer sequence.  \n\nAnalyzing shortest paths:  \n- N7 → N5 → N1 (length 2): immediate end.  \n- N7 → N3 → N2 (length 2): end.  \n- N7 → N3 → N9 → N4 → N8 → N0 (length 4): end.  \n- N7 → N9 → N4 → N8 → N0 (length 4).  \nSo, N9 or N3 → N9 is the longest path.  \nThus, choosing N9 creates a path of 4 moves, which is longer than others.\n\nThus, choosing N9 leads to a longer game and better position control.  \n**Best move = N9.**  \n</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 1.5785795132993774, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N9, N3, or N5.  \n- N9 leads to N4, then N3 or N4 (N4 → N6 or N8) eventually leads to terminal.  \n- N3 → N2 (terminal), N3 → N9 → N4 → N6 (terminal) or N4 → N8 → N0 (terminal).  \n- N5 → N1 → terminal.  \n\nAll moves lead to terminal eventually. So, each path ends after some moves.  \nBut key: we aim to **maximize the game outcome** — the player who forces the opponent into the terminal node (no legal move) wins.  \nAll paths are directed and end in terminals.  \nThe length of paths varies. A longer path allows more moves. Since player who has no move loses, **extending the game (length) increases control** — if the game is longer, the first player can force the opponent to lose in a longer sequence.  \n\nAnalyzing shortest paths:  \n- N7 → N5 → N1 (length 2): immediate end.  \n- N7 → N3 → N2 (length 2): end.  \n- N7 → N3 → N9 → N4 → N8 → N0 (length 4): end.  \n- N7 → N9 → N4 → N8 → N0 (length 4).  \nSo, N9 or N3 → N9 is the longest path.  \nThus, choosing N9 creates a path of 4 moves, which is longer than others.\n\nThus, choosing N9 leads to a longer game and better position control.  \n**Best move = N9.**  \n</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N8\nN7 -> N9, N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1\nN3 -> N2, N9, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN9 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N4. From N4, the player can go to N6 or N8. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N4 (specifically moving to N6 from N4) is optimal. So, the best choice from N9 is to go to N4, which leads to a winning position. Thus, the optimal move is to select N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 0.45492359932088283, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the only legal move is to N4. From N4, the player can go to N6 or N8. N6 leads to terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, choosing N4 (specifically moving to N6 from N4) is optimal. So, the best choice from N9 is to go to N4, which leads to a winning position. Thus, the optimal move is to select N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> terminal\nN4 -> N6, N8\nN7 -> N9, N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN5 -> N1\nN3 -> N2, N9, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN8 -> N0\nN9 -> N4\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N6 and N8. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N6 and N8. N6 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N6 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N6 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N16\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N3, N2, N7\nN13 -> N15\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4\nN2 -> N17, N1, N6\nN12 -> terminal\nN14 -> N11, N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N12, N13\nN10 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2\nN16 -> N9, N0, N14\nN17 -> terminal\nN9 -> N14, N4\nN0 -> N8\nN15 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10\n\nLegal moves: N9, N0, N14assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N16, the legal moves are to N9, N0, or N14.  \n- N9 leads to N14 (to N11 or N5), both of which end in terminal. N14 → N5 ends the game directly.  \n- N0 leads to N8 → N3 → N2 → N17 (end), N1 (then to N10—terminal), or N6 → N12/13 (terminal). All paths eventually lead to terminal.  \n- N14 leads to N11 (terminal) or N5 (terminal).  \nAll moves eventually end. But N14 → N11 or N5 immediately end; N9 leads to longer, but still finite game.  \nTo maximize game length, N9 is best because N9 → N14 → N11 or N5 (end) — still end. However, N9 → N14 → N11 or N5, or back N9 → N4 (terminal), and N8 → N3 → N2 → N1 → N10 — all end. So all paths end.  \n\nSince the game ends on terminal and no move wins, the optimal is to avoid immediate loss. But every move eventually leads to terminal. So, to delay, N9 gives more branching and options before terminal than others. Thus, N9 leads to more potential paths (N14 → N11/N5), so it gives better outcome.  \n\nThus, best choice: N9</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.3900113186191286, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N16, the legal moves are to N9, N0, or N14.  \n- N9 leads to N14 (to N11 or N5), both of which end in terminal. N14 → N5 ends the game directly.  \n- N0 leads to N8 → N3 → N2 → N17 (end), N1 (then to N10—terminal), or N6 → N12/13 (terminal). All paths eventually lead to terminal.  \n- N14 leads to N11 (terminal) or N5 (terminal).  \nAll moves eventually end. But N14 → N11 or N5 immediately end; N9 leads to longer, but still finite game.  \nTo maximize game length, N9 is best because N9 → N14 → N11 or N5 (end) — still end. However, N9 → N14 → N11 or N5, or back N9 → N4 (terminal), and N8 → N3 → N2 → N1 → N10 — all end. So all paths end.  \n\nSince the game ends on terminal and no move wins, the optimal is to avoid immediate loss. But every move eventually leads to terminal. So, to delay, N9 gives more branching and options before terminal than others. Thus, N9 leads to more potential paths (N14 → N11/N5), so it gives better outcome.  \n\nThus, best choice: N9</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N3, N2, N7\nN13 -> N15\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N4\nN2 -> N17, N1, N6\nN12 -> terminal\nN14 -> N11, N5\nN4 -> terminal\nN6 -> N12, N13\nN10 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N2\nN16 -> N9, N0, N14\nN17 -> terminal\nN9 -> N14, N4\nN0 -> N8\nN15 -> terminal\nN1 -> N10\n\nLegal moves: N14, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N14 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4982173174872666, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N14 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}]
