 -> terminal\nN13 -> N3, N8\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN2 -> N9, N13\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N2\n\nLegal moves: N0, N4, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N0, N4, or N10.  \n- N0 leads to N7 or N2.  \n  - N7 → N10 → N12 or N11 → terminal.  \n  - N7 → N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal).  \n  - N7 → N2 → N9 (terminal) or N13 → N3 (terminal).  \n  - N2 → N13 → N3 (terminal).  \n  N0 gives the opponent a win if they play well but is not a losing position.  \n- N4 → N0 → N7 → N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal). So N4 leads to opponent’s win.  \n- N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal) or N11 → terminal. This leads to a loss if the opponent moves next.  \n\nN10 leads to a terminal node. So, player who moves to N10 wins immediately if the opponent has no choice.  \nThus, N10 is a winning move.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.31955291454442564, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N0, N4, or N10.  \n- N0 leads to N7 or N2.  \n  - N7 → N10 → N12 or N11 → terminal.  \n  - N7 → N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal).  \n  - N7 → N2 → N9 (terminal) or N13 → N3 (terminal).  \n  - N2 → N13 → N3 (terminal).  \n  N0 gives the opponent a win if they play well but is not a losing position.  \n- N4 → N0 → N7 → N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal). So N4 leads to opponent’s win.  \n- N10 → N12 → N1 (terminal) or N11 → terminal. This leads to a loss if the opponent moves next.  \n\nN10 leads to a terminal node. So, player who moves to N10 wins immediately if the opponent has no choice.  \nThus, N10 is a winning move.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N10\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0\nN5 -> N0, N4, N10\nN10 -> N12, N11\nN6 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN13 -> N3, N8\nN0 -> N7, N2\nN2 -> N9, N13\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6\nN1 -> terminal\nN7 -> N10, N2\n\nLegal moves: N12, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N12 and N11. N11 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N11 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4922750424448217, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N10, the legal moves are to N12 and N11. N11 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N11 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N11 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N11.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N5, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8\nN6 -> N9, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N6\nN4 -> N3, N7\nN3 -> N2, N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N7.  \n- Moving to N3: path continues through N2 or N8.  \n  - N3 → N2 → N8 (terminal, win for next player)  \n  - N3 → N2 → N2 (no move) → N2 → N8 (terminal)  \n  This leads to terminal nodes for the opponent.  \n- Moving to N7: N7 → N5 or N6  \n  - N5 → N0 or N6 (N0 is terminal)  \n    - N5 → N6 → N9 (terminal)  \n  - N6 → N9 (terminal) or N1 (terminal).  \nAll paths from N7 lead to terminal nodes.  \nBut from N3, the opponent may have to go into N2 → N8, which is terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N3 allows the opponent to be forced into terminal moves.  \nBut from N7, the opponent can move to N6, which leads to N9 or N1. Both are terminal. So, the player who chooses N7 forces the opponent into a losing state.  \nThus, the optimal choice is N7 as it leads to a win for the current player.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3233446519524619, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N3 or N7.  \n- Moving to N3: path continues through N2 or N8.  \n  - N3 → N2 → N8 (terminal, win for next player)  \n  - N3 → N2 → N2 (no move) → N2 → N8 (terminal)  \n  This leads to terminal nodes for the opponent.  \n- Moving to N7: N7 → N5 or N6  \n  - N5 → N0 or N6 (N0 is terminal)  \n    - N5 → N6 → N9 (terminal)  \n  - N6 → N9 (terminal) or N1 (terminal).  \nAll paths from N7 lead to terminal nodes.  \nBut from N3, the opponent may have to go into N2 → N8, which is terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N3 allows the opponent to be forced into terminal moves.  \nBut from N7, the opponent can move to N6, which leads to N9 or N1. Both are terminal. So, the player who chooses N7 forces the opponent into a losing state.  \nThus, the optimal choice is N7 as it leads to a win for the current player.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N5, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8\nN6 -> N9, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N6\nN4 -> N3, N7\nN3 -> N2, N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N5, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5 or N6.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N0 or N6: N0 is terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent gets to move next from N6. From N6, the opponent can go to N9 (terminal) or N1 (terminal). So the opponent wins in this path.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N9 or N1, both terminal. The opponent immediately loses. Thus, moving to N6 is a win.  \nTherefore, moving to N6 is optimal as it leads to an immediate terminal state for the opponent.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4353989813242784, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N5 or N6.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N0 or N6: N0 is terminal (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent gets to move next from N6. From N6, the opponent can go to N9 (terminal) or N1 (terminal). So the opponent wins in this path.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N9 or N1, both terminal. The opponent immediately loses. Thus, moving to N6 is a win.  \nTherefore, moving to N6 is optimal as it leads to an immediate terminal state for the opponent.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N5, N6\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N8\nN6 -> N9, N1\nN9 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N6\nN4 -> N3, N7\nN3 -> N2, N8\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9 and N1. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Similarly, N1 leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player to move wins by forcing the opponent into no move, choosing either N9 or N1 ends the game on the next turn. Both are winning moves. However, since the goal is to maximize the outcome (i.e., force a win), either choice is optimal. But choosing N9 is equally valid as N1. Still, both end the game. As per maximizing outcome, either is fine. However, N1 is a direct terminal move. Given symmetry and no strategic advantage, either is acceptable. But choosing N9 is safe and terminal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4056876061120543, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9 and N1. N9 leads to terminal, so the player who moves to N9 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Similarly, N1 leads to terminal. Thus, both moves end the game immediately. Since the player to move wins by forcing the opponent into no move, choosing either N9 or N1 ends the game on the next turn. Both are winning moves. However, since the goal is to maximize the outcome (i.e., force a win), either choice is optimal. But choosing N9 is equally valid as N1. Still, both end the game. As per maximizing outcome, either is fine. However, N1 is a direct terminal move. Given symmetry and no strategic advantage, either is acceptable. But choosing N9 is safe and terminal.</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN3 -> N1, N6\nN4 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5\nN8 -> N6, N4, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N0, N9\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N6, N4, or N7.  \n- N6 leads to N5 (terminal), N0 (leads to N5), or N9 (terminal). All are terminal, so the player who moves to N6 will force the opponent into a terminal node.  \n- N4 leads to N3, which branches to N1 or N6. N1 is terminal, N6 continues to terminal nodes. So N4 eventually ends in terminal.  \n- N7 leads to N2, which is terminal.  \n\nThus, every move from N8 leads to a terminal state for the opponent. But since the player to move from N8 (i.e. the current player) has full control over choosing a move, and each move leads to the opponent having no legal move eventually, the player wants to force the opponent into a losing position.  \nHowever, every destination (N6, N4, N7) results in a terminal win for the player who moves to the next node. The key is: the player who moves next to a terminal node after an opponent move loses.  \n\nBut all paths from N8 (via N6, N4, or N7) lead eventually to terminal. Since each node transition leads to a chain ending, the player who *moves* from N8 is winning — they move first, then opponent faces terminal.  \nThus, all options lead to a win for the current player, but since we are to pick the *legal destination* that *maximizes* the outcome, and all paths end quickly, all are similar in value. However, in a game with no further strategic gain or draw, choosing a path that leads faster or avoids longer chains may be favorable.\n\nN6 leads to immediate options with N5, N0 (N5 again), N9. All terminal. So N6 forces opponent to end soon.  \nN4 leads to N3 → N1 or N6 — again all terminal.  \nN7 → terminal directly.  \n\nN7 gives the immediate move to terminal state (N2 → terminal), meaning the *next player* will have no move. Thus, if current player selects N7, opponent moves to N2 and is at terminal — so they lose. This implies that moving to N7 forces a loss for the opponent immediately — thus, a win.\n\nAll others force the opponent to take a decision — but N7 leads directly to a terminal node for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N7. This maximizes immediate outcome by forcing the opponent to a terminal node with no move.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.5716751556310131, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N6, N4, or N7.  \n- N6 leads to N5 (terminal), N0 (leads to N5), or N9 (terminal). All are terminal, so the player who moves to N6 will force the opponent into a terminal node.  \n- N4 leads to N3, which branches to N1 or N6. N1 is terminal, N6 continues to terminal nodes. So N4 eventually ends in terminal.  \n- N7 leads to N2, which is terminal.  \n\nThus, every move from N8 leads to a terminal state for the opponent. But since the player to move from N8 (i.e. the current player) has full control over choosing a move, and each move leads to the opponent having no legal move eventually, the player wants to force the opponent into a losing position.  \nHowever, every destination (N6, N4, N7) results in a terminal win for the player who moves to the next node. The key is: the player who moves next to a terminal node after an opponent move loses.  \n\nBut all paths from N8 (via N6, N4, or N7) lead eventually to terminal. Since each node transition leads to a chain ending, the player who *moves* from N8 is winning — they move first, then opponent faces terminal.  \nThus, all options lead to a win for the current player, but since we are to pick the *legal destination* that *maximizes* the outcome, and all paths end quickly, all are similar in value. However, in a game with no further strategic gain or draw, choosing a path that leads faster or avoids longer chains may be favorable.\n\nN6 leads to immediate options with N5, N0 (N5 again), N9. All terminal. So N6 forces opponent to end soon.  \nN4 leads to N3 → N1 or N6 — again all terminal.  \nN7 → terminal directly.  \n\nN7 gives the immediate move to terminal state (N2 → terminal), meaning the *next player* will have no move. Thus, if current player selects N7, opponent moves to N2 and is at terminal — so they lose. This implies that moving to N7 forces a loss for the opponent immediately — thus, a win.\n\nAll others force the opponent to take a decision — but N7 leads directly to a terminal node for the opponent.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N7. This maximizes immediate outcome by forcing the opponent to a terminal node with no move.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> terminal\nN7 -> N2\nN3 -> N1, N6\nN4 -> N3\nN2 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN0 -> N5\nN8 -> N6, N4, N7\nN9 -> terminal\nN6 -> N5, N0, N9\n\nLegal moves: N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N2, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N2 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N2 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4939728353140917, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the only legal move is to N2, which leads to terminal. Thus, the player who moves to N2 wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Therefore, moving to N2 is the only option and results in an immediate win. No other choice exists, so this is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN13 -> terminal\nN5 -> N3, N0\nN2 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\nN12 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1, N11\nN11 -> N5, N8\nN7 -> terminal\nN0 -> N13, N12\nN3 -> terminal\nN1 -> N6, N3\nN8 -> N4, N2\nN4 -> N12, N10\nN10 -> N7, N2\n\nLegal moves: N1, N11assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N1 or N11.  \n- Moving to N1: The next player must move from N1 to N6 or N3. N6 or N3 leads to terminal. So the next player loses.  \n- Moving to N11: From N11, moves are to N5 or N8.  \n    - N5: leads to N3 (terminal) or N0. N0 leads to N13 or N12 (terminal), so this path ends with a win for the player who can force the opponent to a terminal.  \n    - N8 leads to N4 or N2 (terminal). N4 → N12 (terminal), N10 → N7 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). All paths from N11 eventually end in terminal.  \nThus, both N1 and N11 eventually lead to a terminal, but the player who moves to N1 can force an immediate terminal path from the next move. However, N11 allows more options and the opponent can't win immediately.  \nBut crucially: From N1, both moves lead to terminal (via N3 or N6). So if we go to N1, opponent gets a direct terminal after move.  \nThus, choosing N11 gives a more robust, controlling path where opponent is forced into a losing move.  \nHence, N11 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": 1.2944821731748726, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N1 or N11.  \n- Moving to N1: The next player must move from N1 to N6 or N3. N6 or N3 leads to terminal. So the next player loses.  \n- Moving to N11: From N11, moves are to N5 or N8.  \n    - N5: leads to N3 (terminal) or N0. N0 leads to N13 or N12 (terminal), so this path ends with a win for the player who can force the opponent to a terminal.  \n    - N8 leads to N4 or N2 (terminal). N4 → N12 (terminal), N10 → N7 (terminal) or N2 (terminal). All paths from N11 eventually end in terminal.  \nThus, both N1 and N11 eventually lead to a terminal, but the player who moves to N1 can force an immediate terminal path from the next move. However, N11 allows more options and the opponent can't win immediately.  \nBut crucially: From N1, both moves lead to terminal (via N3 or N6). So if we go to N1, opponent gets a direct terminal after move.  \nThus, choosing N11 gives a more robust, controlling path where opponent is forced into a losing move.  \nHence, N11 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}]
