.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN2 -> N0\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N4, N6, N2\nN0 -> N3, N9, N6\nN5 -> N8\nN6 -> N9, N5\nN9 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N4, N6, or N2.  \n- N4 leads to N7, then to terminal → the player who moves to N4 loses.  \n- N6 leads to N9 (terminal) or N5.  \n  - N5 leads to N8 (terminal), so N6 → N5 → terminal. Thus, N6 is a winning move (the opponent has no choice but to move into a terminal node).  \n- N2 leads to N0.  \n  - From N0: moves to N3 (terminal), N9 (terminal), or N6 (which leads to N5 → N8 or N9). So from N0, the opponent can avoid immediate loss, but eventually the player must move to terminal.  \nBut from N1 → N6, the opponent will face a terminal node or move to N5 (N8) or N9 (terminal). No moves are available from N5 or N9, so the opponent loses on their next move.  \nThus, moving to N6 wins immediately.  \nTherefore, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.31615732880588576, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N4, N6, or N2.  \n- N4 leads to N7, then to terminal → the player who moves to N4 loses.  \n- N6 leads to N9 (terminal) or N5.  \n  - N5 leads to N8 (terminal), so N6 → N5 → terminal. Thus, N6 is a winning move (the opponent has no choice but to move into a terminal node).  \n- N2 leads to N0.  \n  - From N0: moves to N3 (terminal), N9 (terminal), or N6 (which leads to N5 → N8 or N9). So from N0, the opponent can avoid immediate loss, but eventually the player must move to terminal.  \nBut from N1 → N6, the opponent will face a terminal node or move to N5 (N8) or N9 (terminal). No moves are available from N5 or N9, so the opponent loses on their next move.  \nThus, 