[2026-08-12 15:01:34] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N9, N2\nN0 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN7 -> N1, N4\nN4 -> N6, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N1, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N1 and N4. N1 leads to N9 or N2, both of which are terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N1 is a winning move. N4 leads to N6 or N0; N6 leads to N5 or N0, and N5 or N0 leads to terminal nodes eventually. However, since N1 leads directly to a terminal node for the opponent, it is a better choice. Therefore, moving to N1 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.5561120543293718, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N1 and N4. N1 leads to N9 or N2, both of which are terminal nodes, so the player who moves to N1 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N1 is a winning move. N4 leads to N6 or N0; N6 leads to N5 or N0, and N5 or N0 leads to terminal nodes eventually. However, since N1 leads directly to a terminal node for the opponent, it is a better choice. Therefore, moving to N1 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N9, N2\nN0 -> N8, N3\nN6 -> N5, N0\nN7 -> N1, N4\nN4 -> N6, N0\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN8 -> N3\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N9 and N2. Both N9 and N2 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, either move ends the game on the next turn. Since the player to move wins if the opponent has no legal move, choosing either N9 or N2 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but the goal is to maximize the outcome, which in this case means securing a win. Either choice achieves this. However, N2 leads directly to terminal, as does N9. Both are equally optimal. But since the problem asks to choose one, and both are valid, either is correct. Selecting N2 is a safe, direct win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.41502546689303904, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N9 and N2. Both N9 and N2 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately because the opponent has no move. Thus, either move ends the game on the next turn. Since the player to move wins if the opponent has no legal move, choosing either N9 or N2 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but the goal is to maximize the outcome, which in this case means securing a win. Either choice achieves this. However, N2 leads directly to terminal, as does N9. Both are equally optimal. But since the problem asks to choose one, and both are valid, either is correct. Selecting N2 is a safe, direct win.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "syste