ant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N0 and N12. N0 and N12 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player to move wins if the opponent has no legal move, choosing either N0 or N12 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but the problem asks to choose one that maximizes the outcome. Either is optimal. However, N12 is a direct win move with no ambiguity. Therefore, N12 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>", "episode_score": 0.4396434634974533, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N0 and N12. N0 and N12 both lead to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to either wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, both moves end the game on the next turn. Since the player to move wins if the opponent has no legal move, choosing either N0 or N12 results in an immediate win. Both are winning moves, but the problem asks to choose one that maximizes the outcome. Either is optimal. However, N12 is a direct win move with no ambiguity. Therefore, N12 is chosen.</reason>\n<answer>N12</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN3 -> N6, N1\nN4 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN7 -> N9, N3\nN9 -> N2, N1\nN1 -> N4\nN8 -> terminal\nN5 -> N0, N8\nN6 -> N5, N2\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N9 and N3.  \n- Moving to N9: leads to N2 or N1.  \n  - N2 → terminal (lose i