[2026-08-12 15:35:17] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> N3, N9\nN2 -> N8, N6\nN5 -> N4, N7\nN4 -> N10, N13\nN9 -> N8, N0\nN10 -> N6, N2\nN13 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N12\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N13, N11\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N10 or N13. N13 ends the game (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move from N10. From N10, the opponent can go to N6 or N2, both of which eventually lead to terminal states. Thus, from N4, the opponent can be forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N7: leads to N1 or N12. N1 leads to N13 or N11 (both terminal), and N12 leads to N3 or N9. N3 and N9 both eventually lead to terminal states. Thus, N7 also leads to terminal states, but the opponent can potentially escape.  \nCareful analysis shows both paths can lead to a win if the current player plays correctly. However, N4 gives the current player immediate control to force a win via N13. Hence, moving to N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.6359083191850594, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N5, the legal moves are to N4 and N7.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N10 or N13. N13 ends the game (win for the player who moves there), so the opponent has no move from N10. From N10, the opponent can go to N6 or N2, both of which eventually lead to terminal states. Thus, from N4, the opponent can be forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N7: leads to N1 or N12. N1 leads to N13 or N11 (both terminal), and N12 leads to N3 or N9. N3 and N9 both eventually lead to terminal states. Thus, N7 also leads to terminal states, but the opponent can potentially escape.  \nCareful analysis shows both paths can lead to a win if the current player plays correctly. However, N4 gives the current player immediate control to force a win via N13. Hence, moving to N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN12 -> N3, N9\nN2 -> N8, N6\nN5 -> N4, N7\nN4 -> N10, N13\nN9 -> N8, N0\nN10 -> N6, N2\nN13 -> terminal\nN11 -> terminal\nN7 -> N1, N12\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N13, N11\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N10, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N10 and N13. N13 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N13 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N13 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4931239388794566, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N10 and N13. N13 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N13 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N13 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N13.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7, N6\nN7 -> N3\nN6 -> terminal\nN4 -> N0, N9, N2\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> N1, N2\nN8 -> terminal\nN9 -> N5, N0\n\nLegal moves: N0, N9, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N9, and N2.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N1 or N2. N1 leads to terminal (win for the opponent), N2 leads to N7 or N6. N7 leads to N3 (terminal), N6 leads to terminal. So from N0, the opponent can force a win if we go to N1.  \n- Moving to N9: leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N8 (terminal), N0 leads to N1 or N2 (same as above). Again, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N7 or N6. N7 → N3 (terminal), N6 → terminal. Both are terminal. So the opponent has no move. This is a winning move.  \nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a losing position. The optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.2889926428975666, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N0, N9, and N2.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N1 or N2. N1 leads to terminal (win for the opponent), N2 leads to N7 or N6. N7 leads to N3 (terminal), N6 leads to terminal. So from N0, the opponent can force a win if we go to N1.  \n- Moving to N9: leads to N5 or N0. N5 leads to N8 (terminal), N0 leads to N1 or N2 (same as above). Again, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N7 or N6. N7 → N3 (terminal), N6 → terminal. Both are terminal. So the opponent has no move. This is a winning move.  \nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a losing position. The optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>