onse": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N0, N10, and N2.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N2, N7, or N12. From N2, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal), N13 (terminal), or N8. From N8, the opponent can go to N4 (terminal). From N7, the opponent can go to N5 (terminal). So from N0, the opponent has no winning move if we go to N0.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N3, then to N11, N13, or N0. N11 → N6 (terminal), N13 → terminal, N0 → N2, N7, N12 (as above). So the opponent can eventually force a win from N3.  \n- Thus, moving to N0 gives the opponent a losing position.  \nTherefore, moving to N0 maximizes the chance of winning.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -0.30851726089417103, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N9, the legal moves are to N0, N10, and N2.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N2, N7, or N12. From N2, the opponent can go to N1 (terminal), N13 (terminal), or N8. From N8, the opponent can go to N4 (terminal). From N7, the opponent can go to N5 (terminal). So from N0, the opponent has no winning move if we go to N0.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N3, then to N11, N13, or N0. N11 → N6 (terminal), N13 → terminal, N0 → N2, N7, N12 (as above). So the opponent can eventually force a win from N3.  \n- Thus, moving to N0 gives the opponent a losing position.  \nTherefore, moving to N0 maximizes the chance of winning.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\