[2026-08-12 17:46:31] [agentic_pipeline.py (513)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 508552] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N7\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN9 -> N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3\nN7 -> N9, N2, N0\nN2 -> N6, N4, N1\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N9, N2, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N9, N2, and N0. N9 leads to N5 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. N2 leads to N6, N4, or N1; N4 and N1 lead to terminal, but N6 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (then to N3, terminal). Thus, from N2, the opponent can be forced into a terminal node. N0 leads to N2, and from N2, the opponent can again move to N6, N4, or N1. N4 and N3 are terminal, but N6 leads to N8 or N1 (both eventually terminal). So, moving to N2 gives the current player a winning strategy. Thus, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.26097906055461245, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N7, the legal moves are to N9, N2, and N0. N9 leads to N5 (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately. N2 leads to N6, N4, or N1; N4 and N1 lead to terminal, but N6 leads to N8 (terminal) or N1 (then to N3, terminal). Thus, from N2, the opponent can be forced into a terminal node. N0 leads to N2, and from N2, the opponent can again move to N6, N4, or N1. N4 and N3 are terminal, but N6 leads to N8 or N1 (both eventually terminal). So, moving to N2 gives the current player a winning strategy. Thus, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2\nN6 -> N8, N1\nN9 -> N5\nN8 -> terminal\nN1 -> N3\nN7 -> N9, N2, N0\nN2 -> N6, N4, N1\nN3 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6, N4, and N1. N4 and N5 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 1.82645727221279, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N6, N4, and N1. N4 and N5 lead directly to terminal nodes, so the player who moves to them wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7\nN9 -> N3\nN3 -> N2\nN0 -> N9, N8, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N8, N5\nN8 -> N4, N1\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N9, N8, and N6.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3, then to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N4 or N1. N1 is terminal, N4 leads to N7 (terminal). So from N8, all moves lead to terminal. The opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N9, N8, or N5. N5 is terminal. From N9 → N3 → N2 (terminal), from N8 → N4 → N7 (terminal). So from N6, the opponent can be forced to a terminal. Thus, from N0 → N6, the current player can force a win by moving to N6.  \nThus, choosing N6 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.26946802490096217, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N0, the legal moves are to N9, N8, and N6.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3, then to N2 (terminal), so the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N4 or N1. N1 is terminal, N4 leads to N7 (terminal). So from N8, all moves lead to terminal. The opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N9, N8, or N5. N5 is terminal. From N9 → N3 → N2 (terminal), from N8 → N4 → N7 (terminal). So from N6, the opponent can be forced to a terminal. Thus, from N0 → N6, the current player can force a win by moving to N6.  \nThus, choosing N6 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> terminal\nN4 -> N7\nN9 -> N3\nN3 -> N2\nN0 -> N9, N8, N6\nN7 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN6 -> N9, N8, N5\nN8 -> N4, N1\n\nLegal moves: N9, N8, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N8, and N5. N5 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.1623372948500283, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N9, N8, and N5. N5 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N5 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N5 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN4 -> N9, N1, N6\nN9 -> N3, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N9\nN6 -> N5, N0, N1\n\nLegal moves: N9, N1, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9, N1, and N6.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3 or N2: N3 is terminal (win for the opponent), N2 leads to N7 (terminal). So from N9, the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N9 (then to N3 or N2, both leading to terminal). So from N1, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N5, N0, or N1. N0 is terminal, N5→N8 (terminal), N1→N9→... (opponent wins). So from N6, the player can choose N0 or N5 (both terminal), thus forcing a win.  \nThus, moving to N6 leads to a winning position. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.2728636106395021, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N4, the legal moves are to N9, N1, and N6.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3 or N2: N3 is terminal (win for the opponent), N2 leads to N7 (terminal). So from N9, the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N9 (then to N3 or N2, both leading to terminal). So from N1, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N5, N0, or N1. N0 is terminal, N5→N8 (terminal), N1→N9→... (opponent wins). So from N6, the player can choose N0 or N5 (both terminal), thus forcing a win.  \nThus, moving to N6 leads to a winning position. The optimal choice is N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> terminal\nN3 -> terminal\nN0 -> terminal\nN2 -> N7\nN4 -> N9, N1, N6\nN9 -> N3, N2\nN7 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN1 -> N9\nN6 -> N5, N0, N1\n\nLegal moves: N5, N0, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N5, N0, and N1. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": 1.1623372948500283, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, the legal moves are to N5, N0, and N1. N0 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N0 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N0 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N0.</reason>\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N2\nN6 -> N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N7, N8assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N7 and N8.  \n- Moving to N7 leads to N9 or N6. N9 ends the game (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately if we go to N9. If we go to N6, then from N6 we can go to N5 (terminal) or N4 (terminal). Both are terminal, so the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N6 or N2.  \n  - From N6: to N5 or N4 (both terminal).  \n  - From N2: to N3 or N4. N3 → N0 (terminal), N4 → terminal.  \nThus, from N8, we can force the opponent into a terminal node.  \nTherefore, moving to N8 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.3980475382003394, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N1, the legal moves are to N7 and N8.  \n- Moving to N7 leads to N9 or N6. N9 ends the game (terminal), so the opponent wins immediately if we go to N9. If we go to N6, then from N6 we can go to N5 (terminal) or N4 (terminal). Both are terminal, so the opponent wins.  \n- Moving to N8 leads to N6 or N2.  \n  - From N6: to N5 or N4 (both terminal).  \n  - From N2: to N3 or N4. N3 → N0 (terminal), N4 → terminal.  \nThus, from N8, we can force the opponent into a terminal node.  \nTherefore, moving to N8 is a winning move. The optimal choice is N8.</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N2\nN6 -> N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N6, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N6 and N2.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N5 or N4. N5 and N4 are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4. N3 leads to N0 (terminal), and N4 is terminal. So from N2, the opponent has no move after we go to N3 or N4.  \nThus, moving to N2 is a winning move.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": 0.45237691001697794, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, the legal moves are to N6 and N2.  \n- Moving to N6 leads to N5 or N4. N5 and N4 are terminal, so the opponent wins immediately.  \n- Moving to N2 leads to N3 or N4. N3 leads to N0 (terminal), and N4 is terminal. So from N2, the opponent has no move after we go to N3 or N4.  \nThus, moving to N2 is a winning move.  \nTherefore, the optimal choice is N2.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N0, N7\nN2 -> N1, N5\nN5 -> N9, N1\nN8 -> terminal\nN0 -> N8, N6\nN6 -> N4\nN3 -> terminal\nN9 -> N3, N7\nN7 -> terminal\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N1, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N1 and N5.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N0 or N7. N7 is terminal (win for the opponent if they move next), N0 leads to N8 (terminal) or N6. N6 → N4 (terminal). So from N1, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N9 or N1. N1 is a losing position as analyzed. N9 → N3 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal. So from N5, the opponent has no move after N9 or N1. Thus, from N5, the current player can force a win.  \nThus, moving to N5 is optimal. The best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4090831918505942, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N1 and N5.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N0 or N7. N7 is terminal (win for the opponent if they move next), N0 leads to N8 (terminal) or N6. N6 → N4 (terminal). So from N1, the opponent can force a win.  \n- Moving to N5 leads to N9 or N1. N1 is a losing position as analyzed. N9 → N3 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal. So from N5, the opponent has no move after N9 or N1. Thus, from N5, the current player can force a win.  \nThus, moving to N5 is optimal. The best choice is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N7, N8\nN7 -> N9, N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN2 -> N3, N4\nN4 -> terminal\nN8 -> N6, N2\nN6 -> N5, N4\nN0 -> terminal\nN5 -> terminal\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N3, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": 1.4990662139219015, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N2, the legal moves are to N3 and N4. N4 leads directly to terminal, so the player who moves to N4 wins immediately (the opponent has no move). Thus, moving to N4 is a winning move. Therefore, the optimal choice is N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}]
